\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Mexico peso slips as market eyes Spain\b0\par\par\uc2 548 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Oktober 2012\par 19:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Hopes for Spanish bailout fail to boost Latam \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  again\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Barclays\uc2  sees buying opportunities in Mexican peso\par \par \uc2 * Mexican peso down 0.21 pct, Brazil real flat\par \par \uc2 (Adds comment, update prices)\par \par \uc2 By Walter Brandimarte\par \par \uc2 RIO DE JANEIRO, Oct 16 (Reuters) - Mexico's pesos slipped on Tuesday while Brazil's real pared gains to end flat as investors awaited signs that Spain will request financial aid, a step seen as crucial to stop the spreading of the euro zone debt crisis.\par \par \uc2 Modestly stronger U.S. economic data and expectations about Spain have been supporting appetite for emerging market assets since euro zone officials said over the weekend that Madrid could request a bailout next month.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, two German coalition lawmakers were quoted as saying Germany was open to a precautionary credit line for Spain, though one of the officials later said his comments had been "over interpreted" in the report and that he was not referring to Spain.\par \par \uc2 The uncertainty left investors reluctant to drive Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  much higher, said Alfredo Puig, a trader at brokerage Vector in Monterrey, Mexico.\par \par \uc2 "The market really thinks (Spain) will be sorted out (with a bailout) in the end, but if there is any real evidence to the contrary, it could be dangerous," Puig said.\par \par \uc2 Concerns that Europe's debt crisis could set off a global financial crisis have repeatedly hit riskier assets around the world since late 2010.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso slipped 0.21 percent to 12.8420 per dollar while the Brazilian real pared early gains to bid flat at 2.0341 per dollar. Chile and Colombia's pesos dipped slightly.\par \par \uc2 The real has been weaker than 2.03 per dollar since Oct. 9. Possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains and support exporters have contributed to the weakness of the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But even in \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 , where the government has refrained from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains have been modest.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso has shed some recent gains and now stands roughly at the same level it was before the U.S. Federal Reserve unveiled a third round of its bond-buying program on Sept. 13, which was expected to drive large dollar inflows to emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 "Since the announcement of QE3, (those gains) have waned, succumbing, in our view, to profit-taking rather than to any re-assessment of risky assets per se," \uc2 Barclays\uc2  analysts said in a report, adding they see buying opportunities for the Mexican peso.\par \par \uc2 "In our view, this suggests still-reasonable entry opportunities for being long the Mexican peso," they said.\par \par \uc2 Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  prices at 1555 GMT:\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                             daily %  year-to-\par                                      change     ate %\par                            Latest              change\par Brazil real                  2.0341     -0.01     -8.14\par                                              \par Mexico peso                 12.8420     -0.21      8.78\par                                              \par Argentina peso*              6.2000      0.00    -23