\par\par \b \uc2 POLL-Mexican peso seen making little headway into 2013\b0\par\par\uc2 739 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Oktober 2012\par 17:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * For poll data see\par \par \uc2 * Peso seen firming to 12.65 per dollar in 12 months\par \par \uc2 * Outlook improves marginally; high prices, ratings upgrade eyed\par \par \uc2 By Michael O'Boyle\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, Oct 4 (Reuters) - The Mexican peso's gains are expected to be limited in the coming year, a Reuters poll showed on Thursday, due to concerns about high asset prices and a weak global economy, but the chance of a ratings upgrade could spur value.\par \par \uc2 The peso , Latin America's most freely traded \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , is seen 1 percent stronger from Wednesday's close by next September at 12.65 per dollar, according to the median of 27 analysts surveyed by Reuters in the last week.\par \par \uc2 About half of analysts see the peso between 12.50 and 13.00 during the next year. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rocketed back from a more than three-year low hit in early June, leaving it up 9 percent so far this year, but most do not see it gaining much more.\par \par \uc2 The median target puts the peso little changed at 12.80 by the end of 2012.\par \par \uc2 Analysts warn that massive U.S. and European monetary stimulus programs may not boost growth while investors are nervous about high valuations in riskier assets, such as Mexican stocks and bonds that are trading near record levels.\par \par \uc2 "Investors are now more conscious that (monetary stimulus) will not necessarily have an economic effect and that it could be creating a bubble, so they are not willing to go all in," said Jorge Gordillo, an analyst at CI Banco in Mexico City.\par \par \uc2 The stimulus plans launched last month by the U.S. Federal Reserve and the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  only helped improve expectations slightly compared to an early August poll.\par \par \uc2 Rock-bottom U.S. interest rates and solid Mexican growth are seen pushing a steady stream of investors toward local assets, but they will be looking to buy on dips rather than push stocks and bonds too far past recent highs, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 The yield on Mexico's benchmark 10-year bond hit a record low in July, but then saw a steep sell off before starting to fall again last month.\par \par \uc2 The IPC stock index also hit an all-time high in July, slumped and is now nearly back to record levels.\par \par \uc2 WIDE RANGE\par \par \uc2 Despite steady median forecasts, targets showed a wide range, with the risk of a weaker peso tilted towards the first quarter of 2013.\par \par \uc2 Nearly one-third of respondents, 8 of 28, see the peso at 13.00 per dollar or weaker in the first quarter of 2013 while 10 out of 27 see it stronger than 12.50 by next September. Only four saw the peso stronger than 12.50 in the first quarter, while five saw the peso at 13.00 or weaker in the third quarter.\par \par \uc2 President-elect Enrique Pena Nieto, who takes office on Dec. 1, has pledged to push for fiscal and energy reforms that could boost growth. If those long-stalled bills are passed, it could spur credit ratings agencies to upgrade Mexico's sovereign debt.\par \par \uc2 Many analysts are skeptical the country's divided Congress will swiftly pass deep reforms, but progress would likely boost the peso and drive it toward 12 per dollar, they said.\par \par \uc2 "Reforms are probable, and that is why we see an appreciation into the third quarter since the market will be speculating on a debt upgrade," said Salvador Orozco, a strategist at Santander in \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  City.\par \par \uc2 Other analysts say increasing \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by other emerging market central banks to fight gains in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will make the peso more attractive since Mexican policymakers avoid meddling in markets.\par \par \uc2 But the ease of trading the peso has made it a magnet for speculators who bet against the peso whenever global risk sentiment sours. Continued worries about Europe and U.S. growth could send the peso back above 13 per dollar, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 "Mexico has become more of a proxy for risk than a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that trades on fundamentals," said Marjorie Hernandez, a strategist at \uc2 HSBC\uc2  in New York. "You still have a lot of volatility and lack of conviction abroad."\par \par \uc2 (For other stories from the poll see )\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Noe Torres and Jean Luis Arce; Editing by Chizu Nomiyama)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 -MEXICO/POLL | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | NAT | U | C | GRO | MTL | SOF | O | OIL   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020121004e8a40010m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
