\par\par \b \uc2 Mexico's Peso Closes Weaker as European Worries Reemerge\b0\par\par\uc2 368 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 2012\par 22:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Peso closes in Mexico City at MXN12.9265; stocks, bonds gain\par \par \uc2 --Renewed worries about Greece affect emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 --Mexico's early September inflation below forecasts\par \par \uc2 By Anthony Harrup\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY--Mexico's peso closed slightly weaker against the U.S. dollar Monday despite gains in local stocks and bonds as Greece and its fiscal difficulties returned to traders' screens, prompting a new bout of risk aversion.\par \par \uc2 The peso was quoted closing in Mexico City at MXN12.9265 to the U.S. dollar, according to Infosel, compared with MXN12.87 at the close Friday.\par \par \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  unit Banamex said in a report that despite peso losses in recent sessions, it expects the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to recover.\par \par \uc2 "We think market participants will see this as offering an entry point in one of the few [emerging market] \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  posing little \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk, as [the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 ] is apparently sympathetic to the role peso strength can play at easing inflationary pressures," Banamex said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has kept interest rates unchanged for more than three years, but talk earlier this year of rate cuts has more recently turned into discussion of the feasibility of rate increases since inflation has been moving further way from the bank's target of 3% plus-or-minus one percentage point.\par \par \uc2 Food prices are the main cause of the quickening inflation, which was running at an annual 4.73% as of mid-September, according to the latest report Monday by the National Statistics Institute. Egg prices were up 38%, bread prices rose 9.5% and tortilla prices were 11% higher from a year before.\par \par \uc2 Overall CPI rose 0.25% in the first two weeks of September, which was below market expectations of a 0.33% increase. "Inflation was better than expected, but food price inflation is a worry," Banamex said.\par \par \uc2 While the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lost ground, local stocks shook off early weakness and closed higher, with the benchmark IPC index up 0.6%. Mexican government bonds gained, in line with U.S. Treasurys, pushing yields down on all but the shortest-dated maturities.\par \par \uc2 Write to Anthony Harrup at anthony.harrup@dowjones.com [ 24-09-12 2054GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1027 1852   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120924e89o000yt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
