\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Latam  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  gains slightly, stimulus eyed\b0\par\par\uc2 615 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 August 2012\par 18:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Investors wait for stronger signs on euro zone plans\par \par \uc2 * Latest EU, German comments fail to ease Latam concerns\par \par \uc2 * Brazil real little changed despite faster growth\par \par \uc2 By Rachel Uranga\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, Aug 17 (Reuters) - Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  edged up on Friday as investors waited for clearer signs of how Europe will deal with its sovereign debt crisis.\par \par \uc2 "The market is stagnant and trading volumes are very low. The world is waiting for September to see if Germany will approve the ECB's stimulus measures," said Jo\u227\'00\'E3o Medeiros, a director at Pioneer brokerage in S\u227\'00\'E3o Paulo.\par \par \uc2 German Chancellor \uc2 Angela Merkel\uc2  on Thursday said a vow by \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief \uc2 Mario Draghi\uc2  to do everything necessary to defend the euro zone is "completely in line" with the approach of European leaders.\par \par \uc2 Merkel's remarks sparked a bounce in European equities, but with no clear plans in place, they failed to inject greater confidence in Latin American markets.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's peso strengthened 0.32 percent to 13.1250 against the dollar, finishing the week down about 0.3 percent. Brazil's real gained 0.19 percent to 2.0145, helped by a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  report showing signs of growth in the country's lackluster economy. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ended the week near flat, stuck in a narrow range.\par \par \uc2 In Brazil, a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  report on Friday showed that the economy in June grew at its fastest pace in 15 months.\par \par \uc2 The bank's IBC-Br economic activity index rose a seasonally adjusted 0.75 percent from May, in line with the median forecast of 18 economists surveyed by Reuters.\par \par \uc2 Investors, however, remained reluctant to buy the real on concerns the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the real slips below 2 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 "Investors are starting to feel like in Brazil the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is overdue for a correction, especially because it does feel like the growth downgrade expectations are bottoming out," said Marjorie Hernandez, a Latin American strategist at \uc2 HSBC\uc2  in New York. "But really what is driving Brazil more than anything is \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk."\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 , data released on Thursday showed Latin America's second-largest economy lost momentum in the second quarter, though Mexico has largely avoided the more extreme slowdown seen in Brazil, buoying investors confidence in the country's outlook.\par \par \uc2 Foreign investment in Mexican debt hit record highs this month, with investors pouring $866 billion into peso debt of three-year maturities or more, according to Aug. 8 data from \uc2 Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The story of strong flows into the Mexican bond market continues, and I wouldn't say it's the strongest argument for Mexican appreciation, but in the short term that's what has been giving it the most support," said \uc2 HSBC\uc2 's Hernandez said.\par \par \uc2 The Chilean peso dipped 0.04 percent in a fifth day of losses. On Thursday the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept its base interest rate steady at 5 percent, on concerns that energy and food prices could grow in coming months. Lower interest rates could make the country's bonds more attractive for foreign investors.\par \par \uc2 Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  prices from Reuters at 1913 GMT:\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                              daily %  year-to-\par                                       change     ate %\par                             Latest              change\par Brazil real                   2.0145      0.19     -7.25\par                                               \par Mexico peso                  13.1250      0.32      6.43\par                                               \par Argentina peso*               6.3000      0.48    -24.92\par                                               \par Chile peso                  483.0000     -0.04      7.52\par                                               \par Peru sol                      2.6120      0.00      3.25\par                                               \par * Argentine peso's rate between                         \par brokerages                                      \par                                                       \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Additonal reporting by Anna Irrera)\f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1) | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | NAT | U | C | O | MTL | GRO | SOF | OIL | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120820e88h000pq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
