\par\par \b \uc2 FACTBOX-Key political risks to watch in Mexico\b0\par\par\uc2 By Dave Graham   \par \uc2 1,303 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 August 2012\par 18:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, Aug 1 (Reuters) - Mexican politics will be in limbo for the next few weeks as electoral authorities examine a legal challenge to Enrique Pena Nieto's July 1 presidential victory.\par \par \uc2 Following are the main political risks to watch in Mexico.\par \par \uc2 ELECTION CONFLICT\par \par \uc2 Andres Manuel Lopez Obrador, the leftist runner-up, has accused Pena Nieto and his Institutional Revolutionary Party (PRI) of buying votes and laundering money during the campaign, and he is demanding the election result be invalidated.\par \par \uc2 The PRI has denied the claims and analysts do not expect the result of the election to be overturned. But the legal challenge has put political deal-making for the next Congress on hold until Mexico's electoral tribunal makes its ruling by Sept. 6.\par \par \uc2 The PRI, which ruled Mexico for most of the 20th century with a mixture of patronage, corruption and authoritarianism, is wary of giving opponents any ammunition with which to undermine Pena Nieto's legitimacy, so they are waiting on the tribunal.\par \par \uc2 Until then, the PRI is likely to hold back on elaborating on Pena Nieto's planned labor, fiscal and energy reforms. The measures will face opposition from the left and could help galvanize the post-election protests against the new president.\par \par \uc2 Congress is due to reconvene on Sept. 1 and the PRI fell short of an absolute majority, meaning that Pena Nieto may have to rely on fringe parties to pass legislation.\par \par \uc2 It is a marked departure for a party that held power firmly in its grip between 1929 and 2000.\par \par \uc2 The PRI is likely to get support for its main economic reforms from the conservative National Action Party (PAN) of outgoing President Felipe Calderon, which finished third in the presidential race.\par \par \uc2 Among the measures being eyed by investors is a plan to open up state oil giant \uc2 Pemex\uc2  to more private investment.\par \par \uc2 PAN support would be needed for far-reaching reforms of the company that require changes in the constitution, though the PRI could also pursue more incremental steps.\par \par \uc2 The PAN, which has joined Lopez Obrador in his electoral complaints against the PRI, is likely to seek concessions from Pena Nieto in return for its support for economic reforms.\par \par \uc2 They could include further political reforms to create a more level playing field in election campaigns, as well as measures to curb the power of trade unions traditionally allied to the PRI.\par \par \uc2 Lopez Obrador, a former Mexico City mayor, also contested the results six years ago when he lost by half a percentage point to Calderon and staged demonstrations that shut down parts of the capital for weeks.\par \par \uc2 Although he lost this year's election by more than 6 percentage points, he has already hinted that he will reject the tribunal's ruling on the election if it does not go his way. It is not clear whether he will call for the same kind of street protests that blocked central parts of Mexico City for weeks after the 2006 election.\par \par \uc2 What to watch:\par \par \uc2 - How Lopez Obrador responds to tribunal ruling.\par \par \uc2 - Popular appetite for staging any demonstrations.\par \par \uc2 - The PAN's willingness to work with the PRI on reforms.\par \par \uc2 DRUG WAR\par \par \uc2 Pena Nieto has said his priority is to reduce the violence that has soared since Calderon launched a frontal assault on drug cartels soon after taking office in late 2006. More than 55,000 people have been killed in drug violence since then.\par \par \uc2 The campaign triggered turf wars across the country, subjecting Mexicans to sickening levels of violence.\par \par \uc2 Critics have speculated the PRI might seek to make deals with drug cartels but Pena Nieto has ruled out any negotiations or truce.\par \par \uc2 There are signs the violence may be easing as Pena Nieto prepares to take office. The Reforma newspaper's tally of deaths linked to the drug war showed some 5,900 in the first seven months of 2012, compared to more than 12,300 during all 2011.\par \par \uc2 Financial markets have not been spooked by the decapitations and day-time shootouts, but worries about the impact on investment and tourism persist.\par \par \uc2 What to watch:\par \par \uc2 - How drug-related violence develops in coming months.\par \par \uc2 - The new government's approach in tackling the cartels.\par \par \uc2 - U.S. reaction or any changes in cross-border strategy to fight drug gangs.\par \par \uc2 ECONOMY\par \par \uc2 Economists are upbeat about the prospects for the Mexican economy in spite of turmoil in Europe that has hit the peso \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , prompting policy makers to say it is undervalued.\par \par \uc2 Calderon has said the economy could grow by 4 percent this year, matching last year's performance. Manufacturing has proved particularly robust with carmakers in Mexico producing record numbers of vehicles during 2012.\par \par \uc2 Still, the finance ministry said the pace of growth probably eased somewhat in the second quarter due to the global slowdown.\par \par \uc2 Inflation quickened to a 1 1/2 year high in early July, breaching the upper limit of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target band, but experts say price pressures are contained despite the weak peso potentially pushing up the cost of imported goods.\par \par \uc2 Fears that Europe's debt woes could spark another global financial crisis pushed the peso to a more than three-year low in early June as investors fled riskier assets.\par \par \uc2 Sharp drops in the peso triggered dollar auctions several times in May under a mechanism that kicks in when the peso falls more than 2 percent in a single session.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  held rates steady at 4.5 percent at its last policy meeting noting that despite inflationary pressures, the risk of a global downturn has increased. Analysts are still of the view the bank will keep rates on hold for now.\par \par \uc2 Despite volatility in the peso, which is used widely as a hedge for emerging market risk, foreign investment in Mexican bonds has continued to climb, reaching record highs in mid-June.\par \par \uc2 What to watch:\par \par \uc2 - Change in \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  interest rate policy.\par \par \uc2 - Signs of capital flight from bond markets.\par \par \uc2 - Ad hoc \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 OIL WORRIES\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's lumbering state oil monopoly \uc2 Pemex\uc2  has managed to stabilize oil production after a sharp decline at its largest fields between 2004 and 2009.\par \par \uc2 The government admits oil output will stagnate at around 2.8 million barrels per day (bpd) for the next 14 years without significant new investment in the sector.\par \par \uc2 The world's No. 7 oil producer now imports about 40 percent of its gasoline and risks becoming a net oil importer if it fails to improve production trends.\par \par \uc2 Calderon's government pinned its hopes on a 2008 energy reform that made the first steps toward opening the state-run industry to more private investment.\par \par \uc2 The first incentive-based oil operating contracts were awarded late last year for small, mature fields, but oil majors are waiting for a promised round of more lucrative projects in Mexico's resource-rich deep waters in the Gulf of Mexico.\par \par \uc2 Pena Nieto and the PAN both say \uc2 Pemex\uc2  reforms must go much further and have pointed to the success of Brazil's state-owned \uc2 Petrobras\uc2  as a model. It trades shares on the stock exchange and has managed to boost efficiency and returns.\par \par \uc2 Pemex\uc2  is hoping the election of Pena Nieto could lead to more autonomy and lighten its tax burden.\par \par \uc2 Mexico has long been reliant on oil revenues to fund the federal budget, putting government finances at risk if prices drop. Even when oil prices are high, \uc2 Pemex\uc2  regularly runs at a loss as the government takes the bulk of its revenues in taxes.\par \par \uc2 What to watch:\par \par \uc2 - Foreign companies' interest in new contracts.\par \par \uc2 - Details on oil reform proposals post-election.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Kieran Murray and Will Dunham)\par \par \uc2 MEXICO-RISKS/ (FACTBOX) | C | D | E | M | O | U | MTL | GRO | SOF | OIL | G | RBN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120801e8810014r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
