\par\par \b \uc2 POLL-Mexico's peso seen firming on exports\b0\par\par\uc2 536 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 August 2012\par 18:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Peso seen firming to 12.80 per dollar in 12 months\par \par \uc2 * European crisis weighs, 12-month outlook weaker than in May poll\par \par \uc2 * For poll data see\par \par \uc2 By Rachel Uranga and Jean Arce\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, Aug 1 (Reuters) - Mexico's peso will likely strengthen a bit in the coming year, backed by U.S. demand for Mexican exports and global demand for the country's local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds.\par \par \uc2 The peso , Latin America's most freely traded \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , is expected to rise to 13.18 against the dollar in three months and to 12.80 per dollar in a year, according to the median forecast of 18 analysts polled by Reuters.\par \par \uc2 That 12-month consensus forecast was a bit weaker than 12.73 polled in May, owing in part to concerns about how long the euro crisis will hold back a long-awaited global recovery in trade.\par \par \uc2 But further monetary stimulus from the United States and European central banks is expected to further depress the paltry yields investors get on fixed-income assets in those countries, driving flows into emerging markets, where interest rates are higher.\par \par \uc2 "In the long term the peso is going to appreciate again because of the rate spread," said Enrique Alvarez, an analyst at IDEAGlobal in New York, referring to the gaping difference between U.S. and Mexican interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has maintained a benchmark rate of 4.5 percent for three years. Investors in the interest rate swap market, as well as economists polled by Reuters, expect policymakers to keep rates on hold into next year.\par \par \uc2 By contrast, the U.S. Federal Reserve has promised to keep its federal funds rate at 0-0.25 percent well into 2014.\par \par \uc2 Investors have continued to buy peso-denominated debt despite wild swings in the exchange rate, and the flows have helped anchor the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The peso has firmed more than 9 percent from a three-year low hit in June.\par \par \uc2 But the peso is seen nearly unchanged from current levels in one month at 13.2573 per dollar, the poll showed.\par \par \uc2 Foreign holdings of local debt have nearly quintupled since 2009 to a record 1.283 trillion Mexican pesos ($96.62 billion), as of July 20, according to the latest \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data.\par \par \uc2 Investors have also been attracted to the hands-off policy of \uc2 Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  compared with that of Brazil, which frequently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets.\par \par \uc2 Further supporting the peso, \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  has held its interest rates steady while Brazil has cut its main rate to a record low to prop up growth.\par \par \uc2 Mexico is on track to outpace growth of its bigger rival Brazil for a second year in a row as strong automobile exports lead solid factory output.\par \par \uc2 A Reuters poll in July that projected a 3.7 percent rate in Mexico and a 2 percent expansion in Brazil this year.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's top trading partner is China, where growth is also slowing, whereas Mexico sends nearly 80 percent of its exports to the United States where consumer demand has held up.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Ross Finley and Andrew Hay)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-MEXICO/POLL | ABN | D | E | RBN | N | U   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120801e8810014b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
