\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Latam  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  gains on China; Brazil gains limited\b0\par\par\uc2 592 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juli 2012\par 18:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Mexican, Chilean peso hit 2-months highs\par \par \uc2 * Brazil's real gains, but limited by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  worries\par \par \uc2 * Brazil's real up 0.1 pct; \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso gains 1 pct\par \par \uc2 By Rachel Uranga\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, July 13 (Reuters) - Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened on Friday after China's quarterly economic data allayed concern of a steep slowdown in the world's second largest economy.\par \par \uc2 Economic growth in China, the world's biggest consumer of raw materials, was in line with expectations, spurring optimism among investors who had feared signs the country was heading toward a hard landing. Gains in the real, however, were limited by fears about further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Brazil.\par \par \uc2 "The market has been looking for some news that is not completely negative, and today we had this idea that the Chinese GDP could have been worse," said Sidnei Nehme, a director at NGO brokerage in Sao Paulo.\par \par \uc2 The sentiment buoyed the Mexican peso, on track to close at a two-month high, up 1.05 percent to 13.3050 against the greenback. The Chilean peso also hit a two-month high, firming 0.65 percent.\par \par \uc2 Chile's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was further bolstered by a more than 2 percent jump in the price of copper, the country's main export product.\par \par \uc2 Both \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  clocked weekly gains with Mexico's peso strengthening about 1 percent and the Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  firming around 1.5 percent. But investors have held back, concerned about the fragile state of the global economy.\par \par \uc2 Investors "are still waiting for further economic data," said Marco Oviedo, an economist at \uc2 Barclays\uc2  in Mexico City, adding that next week's performance will "depend on how investors perceive the global economy."\par \par \uc2 Eyes will be fixed on Europe and U.S. industrial production data, he said, forecasting that the peso would likely trade between 13.20 per dollar and 13.40.\par \par \uc2 In Brazil, the real edged up 0.11 percent to 2.0367 per dollar, but concern about possible government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  weighed.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this week, Brazil cut its key interest rate to a record low in a move to kickstart its sluggish economy. At the same time, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has recently been buying and selling dollars in the local spot market to control volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "There's very little incentive for a hedge fund or a real money guy to bet on appreciation if there is a clear risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap the move," said Diego Donadio, Latin America \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and debt strategist for \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  in Sao Paulo.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been trading within a very narrow range between 2.00 and 2.05 reais per dollar in the past eight sessions.\par \par \uc2 Investors have been holding back bets in Brazil and placing their money in Latin America's second largest economy, Mexico, where the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has a hands-off approach to its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  prices from Reuters at 2004 GMT:\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 