\par\par \b \uc2 Mexico's Carstens says  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  volatility may last into 2013\b0\par\par\uc2 415 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Juni 2012\par 17:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, June 19 (Reuters) - Mexico's peso could continue to be punished "for more than a year" by global market volatility from Europe's deepening debt crisis, Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief said in an interview broadcast on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The peso sank to a three-year low at the start of June on concerns Europe's debt troubles could spark another global financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  surged back to a one-month high on Tuesday, helped by bets the U.S. Federal Reserve could deliver more monetary stimulus.\par \par \uc2 Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Agustin Carstens said it was difficult to see a clear end to the volatility.\par \par \uc2 "I hope it is soon, but we have to be prepared to live with turbulence for some time. It could be a question of more than a year," Carstens told broadcaster Televisa in an interview.\par \par \uc2 "We are going to have volatility in the markets," he said, adding that "the strong fundamentals of the Mexican economy will maintain stability in the medium and long term."\par \par \uc2 On Monday, Carstens said the volatility could last for months.\par \par \uc2 The central banker spoke on the sidelines of the Group of 20 summit of the world's top economies in the resort of Los Cabos, where anxious world leaders are pressing Europe to take bolder steps to stem its debt crisis.\par \par \uc2 Carstens noted the peso was trading around levels where it ended 2011. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been one of the top gainers among emerging markets during the first quarter, but then fell the most of any Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in May.\par \par \uc2 "This year we have not had a net depreciation. This is a adequate level, given the circumstances, but (the peso) has the potential to strengthen," Carstens said.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's peso is one of the most easily tradable emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , making it a target for speculators despite the country's slim, direct links to Europe's economies.\par \par \uc2 Brazil has aggressively protected its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  has held back from ad-hoc \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and relies on a dollar auction program that has only been triggered twice this year.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  held borrowing costs steady and seemed to rule out an interest rate cut any time soon due to concern that a weak peso could fan inflation.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting By Michael O'Boyle; Editing by Andrew Hay)\par \par \uc2 MEXICO-PESO/ | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | NAT | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120619e86j000uf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
