\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  swings may stir debt investors in Mexico, Peru\b0\par\par\uc2 970 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juni 2012\par 20:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  * Mexico, Peru debt mkts most vulnerable to outflows in Latam\par \par \uc2  * Peru acting to curb \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  avoiding \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  * Appetite for emerging mkt debt ebbing after strong inflows \par \par \uc2  By Walter Brandimarte\par \par \uc2  SAO PAULO, June 13 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility has started to sap foreign investors' appetite for the local-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds issued by emerging economies, adding pressure on countries such as Mexico and Peru to limit sharp swings in their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates.\par \par \uc2  Due to their open capital markets, Mexico's and Peru's domestic debt markets are the most vulnerable in Latin America to bouts of capital flight unlike Brazil, whose domestic bond market is much less friendly to foreign investors.\par \par \uc2  "When you look at a sudden reversal in capital flows, you have to look at those countries that have the highest percentage of foreign holdings (in their local debt). And that's essentially Mexico and Peru," said Siobhan Morden, head of Latin America strategy at \uc2 Jefferies & Co\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  "The larger the percentage of foreign holdings, the more that requires policymakers to be responsive and proactive in terms of limiting the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility," she added.  \par \par \uc2  So far, both countries have been resilient in the face of  an incipient flight of foreign capital out of emerging markets. But the Peruvian and the Mexican central banks might be forced to become more active in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets if redemptions grow. \par \par \uc2  Latin American central banks have used a variety of methods to support their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the 2009 crisis, from direct sales of dollars in the spot market to the use of derivative contracts that increase the supply of greenbacks in the futures market. None have resorted to raising interest rates at a time when the global economy is slowing and they need to stimulate domestic consumption.\par \par \uc2  RECORD INFLOWS REVERSE \par \par \uc2  After attracting record inflows in 2011, emerging market bonds denominated in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  lost much of their appeal to foreign investors this year as domestic interest rates dropped and concerns about \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility rose.\par \par \uc2  So far this year, $8.3 billion have found their way into funds that invest in emerging market hard-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, while a more modest $2.8 billion have flown into those investing in local-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt, according to EPFR Global, a fund flow tracking firm.\par \par \uc2  "That's just the opposite story from the previous year," said David Spegel, global head of emerging markets strategy at ING. "Last year, 78 percent of all the inflows into emerging markets debt went into local funds."\par \par \uc2  But warning signs have been raised since last month, when those local-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bond funds started bleeding money. They have lost $721 million in three consecutive weeks of outflows, EPFR data shows.\par \par \uc2  "That has a lot to do with the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility that we've seen spike this year in emerging markets," Spiegel said. "Why hold a local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bond if you think that the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate might go against you? Certainly it's been more volatile."\par \par \uc2  PERU ACTS FIRST\par \par \uc2  Peru, with 57 percent of its fixed-rate debt stock in the hands of foreign investors, has so far managed to avoid a potentially disruptive capital flight by actively curbing volatility in its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2  The Peruvian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month ensured a steady supply of dollars to investors by directly selling the greenback in the spot market, and by offering dollar repurchase agreements for the first time ever.\par \par \uc2  As a result, the Peruvian sol erased all the euro-zone related losses it had incurred in May to trade with gains of about 0.8 percent in the year to date.\par \par \uc2  Mexico's case is different, though. \par \par \uc2  The Mexican peso has weakened some 10 percent since mid-March as policymakers have limited \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a minimum. Their official policy has been to offer up to $400 million in auctions in the spot market whenever the peso declines by at least 2 percent against the dollar in a single session.\par \par \uc2  Still, the fact that there have been no outflows from Mexico's "bonos" suggests many foreign investors might have been hedging out their exposure to the peso, said Jefferies' Morden.\par \par \uc2  Foreigners currently hold a record-high 44.6 percent of Mexico's 1.8 trillion pesos ($128 billion) local-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt market. Analysts are unsure how many of them are protected against \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves, but they say such hedging \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  have driven much of the recent volatility in the peso.\par \par \uc2  Despite that volatility, Mexican policymakers have brushed aside the chance of more aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Agustin Carstens repeatedly affirming that the bank should let the free-floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime function to the greatest degree possible. \par \par \uc2  "Policymakers are reluctant to be more responsive because \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  has rules to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, but I wonder if those rules need to be reevaluated," said Morden.\par \par \uc2  BRAZIL, AN OUTLIER?\par \par \uc2  The Latin American country that has been most active in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market has not acted to safeguard foreign investors' appetite for its debt, but rather to correct what it has called "distortions" in the market.\par \par \uc2  Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has sold more than $7 billion worth of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps to prop up the real since it resumed this type of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month, but the government still imposes a 6 percent financial tax on foreign investors willing to buy its domestic bonds.\par \par \uc2  Foreigners hold some 12.3 percent of Brazil's domestic debt, and Finance Minister Guido Mantega, in his fight against "speculative" money, said last month the government does not care about having more foreign capital in its local bond market. \par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Michael O'Boyle in Mexico City; Editing by Theodore d'Afflisio)\par \par \uc2 LATAM-DEBT/FXVOL | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | NAT | U | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120613e86d0012c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pich