\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Slow and steady Mexico draws equity bets over Brazil\b0\par\par\uc2 1,220 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Mai 2012\par 19:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 * Fund investors eyeing Mexican stocks over Brazil\par \par \uc2 * Mexican economy might be safer bet in market rout\par \par \uc2 * Brazil hit harder in recent sell-off than Mexico\par \par \uc2 By Pablo Garibian and Krista Hughes\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, May 24 (Reuters) - Mexican stocks are drawing investors away from Brazil as Latin America's biggest economy loses steam and Mexico's close ties to the United States render it a safer bet in unsettled times.\par \par \uc2 Although the market capitalization of Brazil's bourse is three times that of Mexico's, the Mexican stock market's standing with fund investors has been on a slow and steady rise since the end of last year, according to data from EPFR Global, which tracks funds with $16 trillion in global assets.\par \par \uc2 Slow and steady economic growth, at an annual rate of 3 to 4 percent, is also earning Mexico a second look from those with money to invest in the region despite renewed fears about the future of the euro zone and the chance of a Lehman-style global crisis.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has resisted the temptation to change interest rates, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets or battle capital inflows in recent years, is in part benefiting from Brazil's unsettling changeability in key policy areas as well as its own ties to the perceived 'safe haven' of the United States.\par \par \uc2 As risk aversion has flared again this year, Brazil's exchange suffered a bigger hit than Mexico's, suggesting investors may be more willing to hold on to Mexican stocks in crunch times. First-quarter foreign inflows into Mexican stocks were the largest since the third quarter of 2009.\par \par \uc2 "Recently I find that we have been putting more money to work in Mexico at the expense of Brazil," said Ed Kuczma, investment analyst for two Van Eck emerging market equity funds which had $275 million assets under management as of April 30.\par \par \uc2 "I think investors are starting to see a kind of top-down, favorable macro environment for Mexico and a more challenging environment for Brazil."\par \par \uc2 After an impressive 7.5 percent expansion in 2010, Brazil's economy, which is twice the size of Mexico's, slowed last year to a modest 2.7 percent, less than half the growth recorded by Peru, Colombia, Chile or even Ecuador.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's economic well-being is closely tied to commodity prices and demand from China, where growth is easing and which would be particularly vulnerable to a collapse in demand from Europe, its biggest export market. Italy and Spain have slid back into recession while France is stagnating.\par \par \uc2 Experts are concerned about the hit to Latin America's economy from a fresh euro zone crisis, a topic to be discussed at next week's Latin America Reuters Summit, which will be attended by the finance ministers of Brazil, Peru, Colombia, Chile and Mexico.\par \par \uc2 While economists have been cutting back growth projections in Brazil, many have upgraded their outlooks for Mexico, where policymakers are optimistic 2012 growth will near 4 percent on the back of stronger U.S. demand for manufactured goods and a view that the United States might hold up in the face of a fresh European crisis.\par \par \uc2 "There's a general sense of Mexico might be more slow and steady, if you like, but more stable at the same time, whereas Brazil has been on this roller coaster ride," said Neil Shearing, economist at Capital Economics in London.\par \par \uc2 "Real strains are starting to emerge in its growth model."\par \par \uc2 Still, EPFR data show fixed income funds are sticking with Brazil, where benchmark interest rates are among the dearest in the world at 9 percent. In real terms, Brazilian rates are about 4 percent, compared to Mexico's 1 percent.\par \par \uc2 TALLEST MIDGET\par \par \uc2 Mexico's stock market is shallow and illiquid compared to many of its emerging market peers, with many big companies controlled by family interests, such as Carlos Slim's \uc2 America Movil\uc2  and baker Grupo Bimbo.\par \par \uc2 But in an emerging market rout, fund managers said Mexico, which has so far escaped an economic hit from its war against drug cartels, would gain from being the least-worst option.\par \par \uc2 "There's so much money around, where are you going to put it?" said a strategist with a major fund, who declined to be named. "It's the tallest midget competition, everyone is tiny ... but you have to choose one, so who are you going to choose? You choose the tallest one."\par \par \uc2 Mexican officials also point to the ease of entry in the country's markets, with no need for foreign investors to register with authorities, unlike Brazil; no withholding tax; no minimum investment period and a freely convertible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "We have a flexible and modern legal framework compared to other emerging markets. This makes it much easier for the foreign investor to take a position in Mexican assets," said Jorge Alegria, head of Mexico's MexDer derivatives exchange.\par \par \uc2 Foreign investment represents 32 percent of the Mexican market's cap. Comparative figures are not available for Brazil, but foreign investors accounted for about 40 percent of share transactions so far in May.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's benchmark Bovespa stock index is down more than 11 percent so far this month, on track for its worst monthly loss since the depths of the global crisis in October 2008.\par \par \uc2 Brazilian stock market data shows that between May 1 and 18 there was a net 2.543 billion reais ($1.21 billion) outflow in foreign investment in equities, although year-to-date data are still positive at $1.29 billion.\par \par \uc2 In Mexico, where the IPC index has fallen more than 5\par \par \uc2 percent this month, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data show foreign investment inflows in company equity of close to $2 billion between January and the end of April.\par \par \uc2 Equity investors have been put off Brazil by a tax on financial transactions by foreigners, one of the measures put in place by President Dilma Rousseff's government last year to tame the then-appreciating real.\par \par \uc2 And many feel the country has been overbought after years of frenzied interest, which drove more than 100 companies to go public between 2006 and 2007 and sent share prices above levels easily justified by economic fundamentals.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's market is seen as a play on China and top oil producers and miners are vulnerable to a downturn in commodity prices while its homebuilders, credit card firms and banks may suffer if credit expansion proves to have been too excessive.\par \par \uc2 "It's very hard to find investment ideas that are not very, very expensive," said Fred Searby, Latin America equity strategist at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 , adding that many Brazilian companies are trading at inflated price to earnings ratios.\par \par \uc2 Brazilian retailer \uc2 Lojas Americanas\uc2  is more expensive than Mexico's \uc2 Wal-Mart de Mexico\uc2  on a price to earnings ratio, and Brazilian brewer Ambev outprices Corona-maker \uc2 Grupo Modelo\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Still, Mexican companies are more expensive than their Brazilian peers in the banking and telecommunications sectors, with Banorte and \uc2 America Movil\uc2  ahead of \uc2 Itau Unibanco\uc2  and \uc2 Telef\u244\'00\'F4nica Brasil\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 ($1 = 2.0940 Brazilian reais)\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Michael O'Boyle in Mexico City, Asher Levine in Sao Paulo, Editing by W Simon)\par 