\par\par \b \uc2 CHRONOLOGY- \highlight22\uc2 Mexico \highlight\uc2 's  \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes \highlight\uc2  as peso slumps\b0\par\par\uc2 403 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Mai 2012\par 19:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 MEXICO\b0 \highlight\uc2  CITY, May 23 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday held its first auction of U.S. dollars since late 2009 after a sharp drop in the peso.\par \par \uc2 Last week, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Agustin Carstens said the peso was trading far below its fundamental value because of the global volatility sparked by Europe's debt crisis, but the bank was happy with its current dollar sales mechanism.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  commission, a joint effort from the finance ministry, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other state agencies, is in charge of setting policy affecting the peso.\par \par \uc2 Following is a list of some of board's key decisions over the past years:\par \par \uc2 October 2008 - Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says it will auction $2.5 billion to stop the peso's most dramatic slide since the so-called Tequila Crisis in the mid-1990s. The bank, which ends up selling $998 million in the market, also announces daily auctions of $400 million whenever the peso declines by at least 2 percent before the dollar during a single session. The amount of that daily dollar auction was trimmed to $300 million in March 2009 and to $250 million in May 2009. The auction was suspended in April of last year.\par \par \uc2 March 2009 - The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  board says it will sell a daily $100 million in a new mechanism to stop the peso's decline. It will coexist with the other daily auction announced in October 2008.\par \par \uc2 April 2009 - The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  gets a swap line from the U.S. Federal Reserve to sell dollars among local financial institutions.\par \par \uc2 February 2010 - The board resumes "put" dollar auctions to add to the country's federal reserves as a measure to cushion the economy from external turmoil. By using this mechanism, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchased $600 million between 1996 and 2001.\par \par \uc2 November 2011 - The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says it will resume dollar auctions to defend the peso, using the same trigger as in 2008,\par \par \uc2 after a slump in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  caused by rising worries of a widening euro-zone debt crisis risks fanning inflation and driving investors from the country's bond market.\par \par \uc2 May 2012 - The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  holds its first dollar auctions since late 2009 after the peso declines sharply.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Adrianna Barrera and Rachel Uranga; Editing by Padraic Cassidy)\par \par \uc2 ABN | D | E | RBN | M | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120526e85n0008g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
