\par\par \b \uc2 *DJ Bank Of Mexico-Government Relations Seen Positive If PRI Returns To Power\b0\par\par\uc2 862 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Mai 2012\par 21:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 4 May 2012 15:14 EDT *DJ  Mexico's Opposition PRI Supports Continued \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Autonomy\par \par \uc2 4 May 2012 15:14 EDT *DJ  Mexico Leading Presidential Candidate's Party Favors Price Stability\par \par \uc2 4 May 2012 15:14 EDT *DJ  Mexico PRI Agrees With \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Policies - Campaign Manager\par \par \uc2 4 May 2012 15:19 EDT DJ  Business As Usual For Bank Of Mexico If PRI Returns To Power\par \par \uc2 --Bank of Mexico's sole mandate is the right path, says PRI campaign manager\par \par \uc2 --Priorities are price stability, avoiding excessive appreciation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 --PRI candidate in agreement with existing exchange policies \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Juan Montes \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MEXICO CITY (Dow Jones)--If Enrique Pena Nieto of the opposition Institutional Revolutionary Party is elected president of Mexico as opinion polls suggest, the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  probably won't have any headaches ahead.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The PRI, as the party is known, ruled Mexico for 71 years until it was unseated in 2000. It now appears to have its best chance in 12 years of returning to power in the July 1 election. Pena Nieto leads second-placed Josefina Vazquez Mota of the ruling conservative National Action Party, or PAN, by more than 20 points, according to recent polls.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We have three goals: first, full respect for the Bank of Mexico's autonomy; secondly, stability of prices as a priority, and thirdly, to avoid an excessive appreciation of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," Luis Videgaray, Pena Nieto's campaign manager and closest adviser, said in an interview with Dow Jones Newswires.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Mexico's principal mandate is to preserve the purchasing power of the Mexican peso, and the bank uses inflation targeting as a cornerstone of its policy objectives. That single mandate has led to some criticism that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  neglects economic growth in favor of keeping prices down, and calls for a dual mandate like that of the U.S. Federal Reserve.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Videgaray said the Bank of Mexico should stick to price stability as the sole mandate. "In a developing country, which could be a victim of speculative attacks from global capital markets, the priority of monetary policy is to protect the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Part of our credibility comes from this, and when monetary policy becomes difficult to predict, that could create some incentives for financial speculation," Videgaray said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Mexico Gov. Agustin Carstens, a former Mexican finance minister and former deputy managing director of the International Monetary Fund, defends balanced public finances as a must, and considers price stability to be the best contribution the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  can make to Mexico's prosperity. He has argued monetary policy can't be a substitute for much-needed energy and fiscal reforms to propel private investment and promote growth, two overhauls Videgaray said the PRI would have as top priorities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Mexico, known for its prudence and orthodoxy, hasn't moved rates since Carstens took over the helm in January 2010 for a renewable six-year term. Carstens was appointed by President Felipe Calderon, of the PAN, and ratified by the Senate with PRI support. The overnight interest rate target has been at 4.5% since July 2009.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Videgaray, who is tipped to become Finance Minister if Pena Nieto wins the election, also expressed agreement with the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Mexico\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's soft \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, responding only to unusual bouts of volatility and allowing the market to determine the exchange rate. Videgaray declined to comment on the possibility of his becoming the next finance minister.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bought dollars last year through monthly put options when the peso was appreciating, and in November, in response to heightened global market volatility, switched to being a potential seller of dollars if the peso weakens 2% in a single day.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Finance Ministry has the last word on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy with its tie-breaking vote in the exchange commission, which is made up of three \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and three ministry officials.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Videgaray, who witnessed major devaluations of the peso under PRI governments in 1982 and 1994, said he would be pragmatic. "A policy based on a free-float is the right path. Mexico has already lived a lot of crises trying to impose the price of its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 JP Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2 's chief Mexico economist Gabriel Casillas said it's improbable there would be any changes under a PRI government that could affect the peso. "In fact, it was the PRI that established the system of flexible exchange rates," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Both Videgaray and Carstens studied economics at Mexico's ITAM university, which in recent decades has turned out a stream of top government officials steeped in economics, and both have doctorates from the U.S.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Both are intelligent economists with a brilliant academic record. So, it would be difficult for conflicts to arise in a relationship that should be basically institutional," said Everardo Elizondo, a former Bank of Mexico deputy governor.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Juan Montes, Dow Jones Newswires; (5255) 5980 5178, juan.montes@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 May 04, 2012 15:19 ET (19:19 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020120504e854005qt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
