\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE:  \highlight22\uc2 Mexico \highlight\uc2  Watches For Effects On Peso From Brazil's  \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 629 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2012\par 21:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  watching exchange rate for effects of Brazilian actions\par \par \uc2 --Official says sovereign upgrade warranted, but unlikely before elections\par \par \uc2 --\b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  considers diversifying \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of reserves, a slow process\par \par \uc2 (Adds comments from Bank of Mexico official, details and background beginning in fourth paragraph)\par \par \uc2 By Juan Montes and Amy Guthrie\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY (Dow Jones)--A Bank of Mexico official said Thursday that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is keeping an eye out for possible peso appreciation as a result of investors shifting money into Mexico as Brazilian authorities take measures to damp down the appreciation of the real.\par \par \uc2 A number of analysts have said the exchange-rate policies of the Bank of Mexico and Mexican Finance Ministry make the peso an attractive option for taking advantage of higher-yielding securities with less risk of exchange \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Jaime Cortina, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said at an event organized by financial information company Profit & Loss that the exchange commission, which is made up of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Finance Ministry officials, is keeping an eye on that and will take it into account.\par \par \uc2 "Certainly, this is something we're monitoring and we're concerned about it," he said.\par \par \uc2 The peso has appreciated around 8.8% so far this year against the U.S. dollar, after a sharp depreciation at the end of 2011 on risk aversion caused by the European sovereign debt problems. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  recently traded in Mexico City at MXN12.8410 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Late last year, at the height of peso volatility, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stopped buying dollars through monthly dollar put options and activated instead a mechanism where it will sell up to $400 million a day on days when the peso depreciates 2% against the dollar from the previous session. So far no sales have been triggered under the mechanism.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's foreign reserves are currently at $148 billion, and Cortina said that reserves will continue to reach new highs as long as oil prices remain high and state oil monopoly \uc2 Petroleos Mexicanos\uc2  changes dollars at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rather than in the open market.\par \par \uc2 The bank is analyzing investing reserves in other emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to secure greater yield, Cortina said, but such a decision would take a long time and central banks must be very cautious to not put reserves at risk.\par \par \uc2 The global trend, and the trend for Mexico, will continue to be diversification away from the U.S. dollar and into other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and assets, such as gold, Cortina said.\par \par \uc2 "The problem is that many of those other markets don't have the size that many central banks require," he added.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  holds some reserves in gold, and has also diversified into \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  other than the U.S. dollar, according to Cortina.\par \par \uc2 Mexico deserves a better credit rating given the country's healthy public finances, with low budget deficit and debt, and record-high international reserves, Cortina also said.\par \par \uc2 "Clearly, there's margin for an upward revision. But I don't see the international credit agencies doing anything before July's presidential election."\par \par \uc2 Mexico has a lower medium-grade credit rating, despite having a budget deficit of 2.5% of gross domestic product in 2011 and a net debt of 32.4% of the GDP, relatively low compared with U.S., Europe and other developed countries.\par \par \uc2 Standard and Poor's sovereign rating for Mexican debt is BBB, similar to that of Fitch, while \uc2 Moody's\uc2  has Mexico one equivalent notch higher at a Baa1. All three agencies maintain a stable outlook.\par \par \uc2 -By Juan Montes and Amy Guthrie, Dow Jones Newswires; (5255) 5980 5178, mexico@dowjones.com [ 29-03-12 1925GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120329e83t001cr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
