\par\par \b \uc2 Bank Of  \highlight22\uc2 Mexico \highlight\uc2  Keeps Eye On Peso Gains From Brazil  \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention \highlight\uc2  - Official\b0\par\par\uc2 316 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2012\par 18:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 MEXICO\b0 \highlight\uc2  CITY (Dow Jones)--A Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said Thursday that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is keeping an eye out for possible peso appreciation as a result of investors shifting money into Mexico as Brazilian authorities take measures to damp down the appreciation of the real.\par \par \uc2 A number of analysts have said the exchange-rate policies of the Bank of Mexico and Mexican Finance Ministry make the peso an attractive option for taking advantage of higher-yielding securities with less risk of exchange \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Jaime Cortina, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said at an event organized by financial information company Profit & Loss that the exchange commission, which is made up of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Finance Ministry officials, is keeping an eye on that and will take it into account.\par \par \uc2 The peso has appreciated around 8.8% so far this year against the U.S. dollar, after a sharp depreciation at the end of 2011 on risk aversion caused by the European sovereign debt problems. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  recently traded in Mexico City at MXN12.8410 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Late last year, at the height of peso volatility, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stopped buying dollars through monthly dollar put options and activated instead a mechanism where it will sell up to $400 million a day on days when the peso depreciates 2% against the dollar from the previous session. So far no sales have been triggered under the mechanism.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's foreign reserves are currently at $148 billion, and Cortina said that reserves will continue to reach new highs as long as oil prices remain high and state oil monopoly \uc2 Petroleos Mexicanos\uc2  changes dollars at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rather than in the open market.\par \par \uc2 -By Juan Montes, Dow Jones Newswires; (5255) 5980 5178, juan.montes@dowjones.com [ 29-03-12 1701GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120329e83t001a4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
