\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-EMERGING MARKETS-Latam  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  weakens as Bernanke enthusiasm eases\b0\par\par\uc2 658 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2012\par 17:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Repeats to additional subscribers)\par \par \uc2 * Mexico peso weakens 0.3 pct, Brazil real 0.08 pct\par \par \uc2 * Brazil cenbank holds reverse swap auction\par \par \uc2 * Traders say Brazil cenbank may be defending 1.81 level\par \par \uc2 By Caroline Stauffer\par \par \uc2 RIO DE JANEIRO, March 27 (Reuters) - Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  mostly ended weaker on Tuesday as enthusiasm for U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke's commitment to loose monetary policy eased and fears of Brazil's desire to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hurt the real.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened as much as 0.3 percent early in the session as Bernanke's comments from Monday lifted higher-risk \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that benefit from low interest rates in the United States.\par \par \uc2 But the real ended 0.08 percent weaker, bidding 1.8162 per dollar after Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold 41,200 reverse \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps in an auction. The swaps are derivatives that mimic the purchase of dollars in futures markets and are used to prevent the real from strengthening.\par \par \uc2 Investors are convinced Brazil's government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will do everything they can to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from appreciating beyond 1.8 per dollar, with some traders saying the bank's target level is now a weaker 1.81 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 "That's what the traders say and the way the bank is \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  suggests it, though I still believe 1.8 is the level," said 4Cast Analyst Pedro Tuesta.\par \par \uc2 Some traders said they feared Brazil's government, which has previously raised the tax known as IOF on financial transactions and extended its scope to foreign debt maturing in up to five years, could take additional measures to weaken the real.\par \par \uc2 "It's hard to think that they'll go beyond what they have in place in a worst-case scenario they might adjust the IOF on derivatives," said Flavia Cattan-Naslausky of RBS.\par \par \uc2 Other countries are also \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, determined to deter hot money inflows from the developed world that have been fueled by the very policies Bernanke pledged to continue.\par \par \uc2 Colombia's peso weakened 0.16 percent to close bidding 1761 per dollar the day after the country's finance minister said the government wants to see more "aggressive" action to help stem \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains and will ask the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to consider stepping up dollar purchases.\par \par \uc2 Peru's sol ended 0.08 percent stronger at 2.668 per dollar even after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought $42 million in the spot market in a routine \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso, which had gained the most among Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Bernanke's comments in the previous session, lost 0.3 percent to 12.7082.\par \par \uc2 Bernanke said on Monday that accommodative monetary policy would stay in place to support demand and that the U.S. economy needed to grow more quickly to reduce the unemployment rate.\par \par \uc2 Some market participants took that as a sign the Fed could launch another round of asset buying, or quantitative easing, to inject cash into markets that in past years have fled to Latin America's fast-growing economies and high-yielding assets.\par \par \uc2 But that interpretation was questioned on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 "The peso saw losses due to market expectation that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  will not launch more monetary expansion," said Economist Gabriela Siller of Mexico-based Banco Base in a note to clients.\par \par \uc2 Asked on Tuesday about the potential for a new round of asset purchases by the Fed, Bernanke told ABC News the Fed does not take any options off the table and needs to be prepared to respond however the U.S. economy evolves.\par \par \uc2 Chile's peso ended nearly unchanged against the dollar, as prices for its main export, copper, were little changed. The peso closed a marginal 0.02 percent firmer, to bid at 486.00 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Dan Grebler)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1) | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | NAT   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120330e83r0013o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
