\par\par \b \uc2 Mexican Peso Closes Slightly Weaker After US Sentiment Data\b0\par\par\uc2 328 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2012\par 22:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakens 0.1% against the U.S. dollar\par \par \uc2 --Weaker-than-expected U.S. consumer-sentiment data weighs\par \par \uc2 --Bank of Mexico stays on hold, offering a brighter growth outlook\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY (Dow Jones) -- The Mexican peso weakened slightly Friday against the U.S. dollar, ending the week mostly flat after worse-than-expected data on consumer confidence in world's largest economy and Mexico's main trade partner.\par \par \uc2 The peso was quoted closing in Mexico City at MXN12.67 to the dollar, according to Infosel, compared with MXN12.6590 at the close Thursday.\par \par \uc2 In the U.S., the Reuters/University of Michigan consumer-sentiment index declined, contrary to economists' forecasts for a gain. Mexico is highly dependent on the U.S. economic prospects, as it sends almost 80% of its exports north of the border.\par \par \uc2 Recent positive U.S. manufacturing and employment data have been supporting the peso, which has appreciated more than 9% since the beginning of the year. "A continued acceleration of the U.S. data and overall economic recovery, could push the peso towards MXN12.20 fair-value level, where the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said \uc2 BBVA\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Claudia Ceja.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the Bank of Mexico said the country's growth prospects have improved "in recent weeks," due mainly to the better-than-expected performance of the U.S. economy and the lower probability of a financial catastrophe coming from the euro zone. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left rates unchanged at 4.5%, as was unanimously expected, and most economists keep expecting the bank to stay on hold for a long period of time.\par \par \uc2 Next week, the macroeconomic focus will be on Mexico's inflation data for the first two weeks of March, scheduled to be released on Thursday at 9 a.m. EST.\par \par \uc2 -By Juan Montes, Dow Jones Newswires; (5255) 5980 5178; juan.montes@dowjones.com [ 16-03-12 2108GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1027 1852   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120316e83g0013a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
