\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Brazil real rises despite cenbank, rates jump\b0\par\par\uc2 684 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Februar 2012\par 16:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Updates with Brazil \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  peso falling)\par \par \uc2 * Real gains despite 2 \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  auctions to buy dlrs\par \par \uc2 * Brazil neutral interest rates seen higher than expected\par \par \uc2 * Mexican peso weakens on euro-zone concerns\par \par \uc2 By Jos\u233\'00\'E9 de Castro\par \par \uc2 Feb 24 (Reuters) - Brazil's real rose on Friday as persistent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  failed to fully absorb dollar inflows, while the country's interest-rate futures rose sharply after a survey suggested the economy needed tighter-than-expected monetary policy to grow without inflation.\par \par \uc2 Most Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were supported by commodity gains, with expectations of higher interest rates further boosting the Colombian peso. However, worries about the euro-zone crisis weighed on the Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real trimmed some of its earlier gains but still traded 0.3 percent higher at 1.705 per dollar after two dollar-buying auctions launched by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  less than one hour apart.\par \par \uc2 The bank's action came one day after it offered to sell reverse \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps for the first time since August and also bought dollars in the spot market.\par \par \uc2 The real has gained more than 9 percent so far this year and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market added to the perception that the government is drawing a line at 1.7 reais per dollar.\par \par \uc2 "The psychological level has inevitably shaped around the 1.7000 handle in spot, though clearly the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is struggling to absorb the large debt inflows related primarily to external corporate debt issuance and overall strong capital account inflows," said Flavia Cattan-Naslausky, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at RBS Securities.\par \par \uc2 At Friday's auctions, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  initially bought dollars at a cutoff price 1.7059 reais, and then at 1.7057 reais.\par \par \uc2 INTEREST RATES JUMP\par \par \uc2 Concerns about the pace of monetary policy easing in Brazil increased after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  released a survey showing economists estimate the economy can grow without inflationary pressures if real interest rates are about 5.5 percent - higher than the 4.5-5.0 percent expected by many traders.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's benchmark interest rate is now at 10.5 percent , after four, half-percentage-point rate cuts since August. Inflation for the 12 months ending Jan. 31 was 6.22 percent, giving Brazil a real interest rate of about 4.3 percent, more than a percentage point below the neutral rate survey estimate.\par \par \uc2 "There is this feeling that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might be exaggerating and, just to be sure, markets raised the rates," said a trader in Sao Paulo.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's interest-rate contract for January 2013 rose to 9.270 percent from 9.220 percent in Thursday's adjusted close. The contract for January 2014 jumped to 9.750 percent from 9.670 percent.\par \par \uc2 COMMODITIES SUPPORT LATAM \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Strong dollar inflows have provided powerful tailwinds to Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year, and analysts expect that trend to intensify as commodity prices rise and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  floods the markets again with cheap money next week.\par \par \uc2 The Chilean peso rose as much as 0.7 percent during the session before ending broadly flat against the dollar, at 480.30 per dollar, as local dollar purchases offset a jump in prices of copper, the country's main export.\par \par \uc2 The Colombian peso gained 0.2 percent to 1,773.05 per greenback. Boosting the prospects for the Colombian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely increase the key interest rate for a second straight month at its meeting on Friday, in an attempt to ease economic growth and cool the pace of bank lending.\par \par \uc2 In Mexico, however, the peso weakened 0.4 percent to 12.876 per dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been unable to break past 12.65 per dollar, trading in a narrow range this week on persistent concerns over the future of the euro zone debt crisis.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Nelson Bocanegra in Bogota, writing by Walter Brandimarte; Editing by Theodore d'Afflisio)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1) | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | NAT | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120227e82o00105\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
