\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Latam  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  rise to multimonth highs\b0\par\par\uc2 494 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Februar 2012\par 22:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Recasts throughout; updates to close)\par \par \uc2 * Mexico, Chile pesos hit 4-1/2-month highs\par \par \uc2 * Brazil's real hits 3-month high, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  eyed\par \par \uc2 * Brazil real firms 0.67 pct, \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  peso up 0.69 pct\par \par \uc2 By Michael O'Boyle\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, Feb 2 (Reuters) - Mexico's and Chile's peso firmed to their strongest in 4-1/2 months on Thursday, while Brazil's real hit a three-month high, as expectations of low U.S. interest rates fueled demand for higher-yielding assets.\par \par \uc2 Easing fears that Europe's debt crisis could spark another global financial debacle have driven global investors into Latin America, and the region's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have led gains among emerging market assets this year.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke defended low interest rate policies before Congress on Thursday, saying the economy of the United States still needs plenty of support.\par \par \uc2 Last week, the Fed said U.S. overnight interest rates would likely remain near zero through late 2014, pushing even more investors to pile into higher-yielding Latin American local-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt.\par \par \uc2 "The added impulse for yield hunting is starting to take the top seat among the different elements that are affecting the market and that is helping Latin America," said Enrique Alvarez, an analyst at IDEAglobal in New York.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's peso firmed 0.69 percent to 12.8010 per dollar while Chile's peso surged 1.42 percent stronger to 479.90 pesos after the cost of dollars in pesos fell below its 200-day simple moving average.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's real bid 0.67 percent stronger at 1.7218 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 Riskier assets around the world have gained as the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  calmed fears of a full-blown euro crisis by pumping 489 billion euros of cheap three-year loans into the banking system since December.\par \par \uc2 A Reuters poll showed on Thursday that further gains by Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be limited in the coming months, but its also indicated that Mexico's peso is best-placed to benefit from another asset-buying program by the Fed.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real is likely to fall back to 1.75 per dollar this month as investors fear another bout of strong official action to protect local manufacturers, which have struggled to sell abroad as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened.\par \par \uc2 Traders are closely eyeing the 1.70 reais per dollar level that was seen spurring \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past.\par \par \uc2 Further easing fears about Europe on Thursday, Chinese Premier Wen Jiabao said China is "considering increasing its participation in the solution of the European debt crisis" through the region's bailout fund.\par \par \uc2 "Those comments from China have helped ease market worries," said Reginaldo Galhardo, exchange trade manager for Treviso Corretora in Sao Paulo. "This optimism is bringing investors back to emerging markets."\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Alonso Soto and Silvio Cascione in Sao Paulo; Editing by Kenneth Barry)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120205e822000oc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
