\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Brazil real slides on trade lines; Mexico level\b0\par\par\uc2 715 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Dezember 2011\par 14:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Expiring trade lines, credit crunch pressure Brazil real\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  concern pushes Brazil real selling\par \par \uc2 Updates prices, adds \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  peso comment)\par \par \uc2 By Jeb Blount and Rachel Uranga\par \par \uc2 RIO DE JANEIRO/MEXICO CITY , Dec 14 (Reuters) - Brazil's real slipped to a three-week low against the U.S. dollar on Wednesday as investors sought greenbacks to pay trade-finance credit lines unlikely to be renewed as Europe's debt crisis prompts banks to limit lending.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's real led a general slide in Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , shedding 1.24 percent to 1.8727 to the dollar. Earlier it weakened as much as 1.36 percent to 1.8750, its weakest intraday level since Nov. 25. Mexico's peso weakened 0.40 percent to 13.9202.\par \par \uc2 The real's declines were likely amplified as some investors used the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a way to reduce their exposure to Latin American risk without causing Mexico's peso, the most-traded regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , from falling enough to trigger an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said Diego Donadio, Latin America \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  in Sao Paulo.\par \par \uc2 "Not only do you have trade lines maturing with the prospect of higher rates and lower availability, you have the Mexican factor," he said.\par \par \uc2 Because of Mexico's Virgin of Guadalupe holiday on Monday, Mexico's official peso fixing rate showed only a 1.06 percent slide on Tuesday from Friday's fix, the previous business day's official rate.\par \par \uc2 That loss was short of the 2 percent loss required for Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to enter and buy $400 million of pesos, an act that could have caused losses for those betting against the Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Donadio said.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the spot rate slipped more than 2 percent when Monday's offshore, nonlocal trading was included.\par \par \uc2 "In a climate where risk aversion is high, it made more sense to sell the real than to sell the peso," Donadio said. "If people had sold the peso as heavily as the real it would have rapidly hit the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  limit."\par \par \uc2 Donadio expects all the region's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slide in coming days as investors continue to buy the dollar and dollar-denominated assets instead of Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and local-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  assets, which are considered riskier than dollars.\par \par \uc2 In addition to the need for companies and investors to pay down year- and month-end credit lines, many want to hold dollars to protect against the risks related to a European debt crisis that threatens to push the world economy into recession.\par \par \uc2 The real, Donadio said, is likely to slide to 1.90 or 1.91 in the coming days and weeks, levels that will then likely spark a response from Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , concerned that a weaker real could stoke inflation or make it difficult for some Brazilian companies to pay dollar debts.\par \par \uc2 "The market is going to test the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  at around 1.90 because it believes the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has made it clear that it won't tolerate bigger weakening," he said.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's peso will likely find resistance near 14.10 to the dollar based on Fibonacci retracement measures, he said.\par \par \uc2 Cesar Elizalde, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at Banorte-IXE, believes the peso could easily pass the 14 per dollar mark, thanks to European concerns and the failure of the U.S. Federal Reserve to take stronger action to prevent Europe's problems from hurting the United States.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, Italian 10-year debt yields, a proxy for government long-term borrowing costs, rose to a record 7.3 percent.\par \par \uc2 "There is no clear definition to the problem of the crisis in Europe and there is worry about the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , so this is the principal problem, the environment and a bit of liquidity," Elizalde said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely, he said, as investors appear to be increasing their bets on a stronger peso in the futures markets.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere in Latin America, Chile's peso weakened 1.19 percent to 521.20 to the dollar, its weakest since Nov. 30. Colombia's peso weakened 0.42 percent to 1,937.60.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Dan Grebler)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111217e7ce000fa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
