\par\par \b \uc2 Mexico November Consumer Confidence 89.5 Versus 88.5 Year Ago\b0\par\par\uc2 304 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Dezember 2011\par 15:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY -(Dow Jones)- Mexico's consumer confidence rose in November from a year earlier, but fell from the previous month in seasonally adjusted terms, the National Statistics Institute, or Inegi, said Monday.\par \par \uc2 Inegi said its consumer confidence index was 89.5 last month, up modestly from 88.5 in November 2010 but down 0.78% seasonally adjusted from October.\par \par \uc2 Compared with a year ago, consumers were less upbeat about the current economic situation and their ability to buy big-ticket items, while more confident about economic prospects for a year from now.\par \par \uc2 Banco Santander\uc2  said in a report ahead of the release that confidence generated by jobs growth and credit expansion in Mexico has been offset by discouraging news on the international economic front, and particularly the peso's depreciation against the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 In November, the peso fell to its weakest level since March 2009, trading above MXN14 to the dollar as a result of global risk aversion generated by uncertainty about the future of the euro. The peso has recovered ground in the past week after the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Finance Ministry announced plans to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from sharply declines.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Mexico suspended its dollar purchases through monthly auctions of dollar put options and instead will sell up to $400 million on days when the peso is 2% weaker than the previous session.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  shift from a buyer to a potential seller of dollars contributed to peso gains the past week. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was recently quoted in Mexico City at MXN13.4895 to the dollar, compared with MXN13.5925 at the close Friday.\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Harrup, Dow Jones Newswires; (5255) 5980-5176, anthony.harrup@dowjones.com [ 12-05-11 0942ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020111205e7c50005u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
