\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Mexico Intervention \highlight\uc2  Kick-starts Peso Turnaround\b0\par\par\uc2 610 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Dezember 2011\par 18:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global FX & Fixed Income News\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --\b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso likely to see further gains in medium-term\par \par \uc2 --\b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says it will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when peso falls 2% against the dollar on the day\par \par \uc2 --The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still susceptible to volatility inspired by risk aversion\par \par \uc2 By Erin McCarthy\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's surprise pledge to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in support of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should hasten the peso's long-awaited turnaround.\par \par \uc2 For the first time in three years, Mexico's typically conservative \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  took steps this week to steady its wildly swinging \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Until its announcement on Tuesday, the Bank of Mexico had been one of a dwindling number of emerging-market central banks to stand by as its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value plunged. On Friday, the peso hit its weakest point against the dollar since March 2009, having fallen about 22% since August.\par \par \uc2 "It was a huge policy decision and it was a credible one because it was so not like them," said RBS strategist Flavia Cattan-Naslausky.\par \par \uc2 Analysts point to MXN14 against the dollar as a likely line in the sand for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, the peso traded at MXN13.5584 against the dollar, more than 2% stronger since the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  announcement, according to CQG.\par \par \uc2 It looks like the start of a rally long anticipated by investors and analysts.\par \par \uc2 Before the announcement, trading in the peso had been a "one-way street down," said Michael Woolfolk, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of New York Mellon in New York.\par \par \uc2 The peso's a popular pick for investors looking to buy and sell emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , due to its high trading volume and the ability to trade it freely throughout the global day.\par \par \uc2 But now investors who are short the peso may be more worried about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks, and may want to protect their portfolios ahead of year-end, said Michael Lee, a portfolio manager at Wells Fargo Asset Management.\par \par \uc2 BNY Mellon's Woolfolk sees the peso ending the year around MXN13.50 to the dollar, and strengthening to MXN12.5 in mid-2012.\par \par \uc2 The peso's also got more room to gain, as it has lagged other emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year. Some investors say the peso is undervalued by as much as 15%.\par \par \uc2 But investors and analysts warn that the peso could see some bumps along the way. A global economic downturn, or financial turmoil in Europe, could spur investors to sell the peso even in the face of any \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "It doesn't mean that we're in a one-way directional trade, because we're still susceptible to the headline risks," said RBS's Cattan-Naslausky.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Mexico said Tuesday it will sell as much as $400 million a day in the exchange market on days when the peso is 2% weaker against the dollar than the previous session, the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  commission said on Tuesday. It also cancelled its monthly auctions of dollar put options, which previously took advantage of peso appreciation to boost dollar reserves.\par \par \uc2 Mexico primarily wants to stem sharp swings in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , since sudden ups and downs can turn investors off of peso-denominated assets, such as local-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds. A steep fall in the peso would also lead to an uptick in inflation.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Mexico has plenty of firepower to stand behind the plan, with nearly $140 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and a $73 billion flexible credit line with the International Monetary Fund.\par \par \uc2 -By Erin McCarthy, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-2712; erin.mccarthy@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 -Prabha Natarajan contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -0- \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 12-01-11 1203ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020111201e7c100047\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
