\par\par \b \uc2 Bank Of  \highlight22\uc2 Mexico \highlight\uc2  Seen Holding Rates Despite  \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention \highlight\uc2  Plan\b0\par\par\uc2 639 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Dezember 2011\par 20:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --\b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  seen keeping rate at 4.5% this week\par \par \uc2 --Measures to stabilize peso make room for future rate cut\par \par \uc2 --Peso's 16% slide vs dollar since July could impact inflation\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par   By Juan Montes \par   Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MEXICO CITY (Dow Jones)--The Bank of Mexico is widely expected to leave interest rates unchanged Friday, despite recent measures taken by local monetary and financial authorities to curb the depreciation of the Mexican peso.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fifteen of the 17 analysts consulted by Dow Jones Newswires still think the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will leave its overnight lending rate target at 4.5%--where it has stood since July 2009--when it publishes its decision and monetary policy statement at 10 a.m. EST Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A heavily depreciated peso was one of the reasons behind analysts' expectations for the bank to stay on hold at its last meeting of the year. This week, however, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Mexico\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  commission announced an \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  measure to prevent the peso from charting sharp single-session losses.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Mexico will sell as much as $400 million a day when the peso is 2% weaker than the previous session, the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  commission said on Tuesday. The peso rallied on the decision and now is trading at MXN13.6035, after trading above MXN14 Monday, its weakest level in 2 1/2 years.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "After the announcement, there have been some rumors among analysts and traders about an increasing possibility of a rate cut Friday, but we still think the Bank of Mexico won't act," said Sergio Martin, \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2 's chief economist for Mexico.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Martin explained the Mexican economy is still growing at a steady pace and inflation pressures are under control, so an interest rate movement wouldn't make sense right now even though the peso has recovered some ground after the exchange commission announcement.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Mexican economy gained momentum in the third quarter, growing 4.5% versus the year-ago period, showing strong domestic demand that could offset a slowdown in industrial output.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 JP Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2 's chief Mexico economist, Gabriel Casillas, said this week's peso appreciation could be short lived. "We do not believe the significant depreciation of the peso that we have observed in the past few months will be reverted in the next few days. Even if this happens, we do not believe Bank of Mexico members would react to short-term dynamics," he explained.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The peso remains about 16% weaker than its levels at the end of July, before the European debt crisis sparked a surge in global risk aversion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It is still early to assess the impact of the [Tuesday] announcement on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," added \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2  analyst Alonso Cervera. "Probably the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will still want to get more clarity as to the unfolding European crisis."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stepping away from the pack, Nomura analyst Benito Berber changed his call and now expects the Bank of Mexico to cut rates after Wednesday's coordinated action by the world's largest central banks to provide liquidity to the financial system.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Berber is expecting a 25 basis point cut Friday and perhaps one more in the first quarter of 2012.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The door has been opened for the Mexican \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to cut its policy rate. It looks like the decision to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  [in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Mexico\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ] could be part of a broader strategy," Berber said in a report.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Mexico has a reputation of being prudent and avoiding hasty policy decisions. In early November, Mexican \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Governor Agustin Carstens suggested the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  won't put peso stability at risk by cutting rates, as the exchange rate could impact inflation in the future.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Juan Montes, Dow Jones Newswires; (5255) 5980 5178, juan.montes@dowjones.com [ 01-12-11 1909GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020111201e7c10017n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
