\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ: Mexico's  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  To Back Peso\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Harrup And David Luhnow   \par \uc2 Of THE WALL STREET JOURNAL   \par \uc2 677 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 November 2011\par 02:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY--Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will begin selling dollars to support the peso on days the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  comes under heavy pressure, a move that shows how Europe's financial crisis is forcing even the most orthodox central banks to become more active in the markets.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Mexico will sell as much as $400 million a day in the exchange market on days when the peso is 2% weaker than the previous session, the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  commission said on Tuesday. The commission is made up of members of the Bank of Mexico and the Finance Ministry.\par \par \uc2 The decision to reinstate dollar sales, which hadn't been used here since the last global financial crisis in 2008-2009, comes after the peso recently fell to its weakest level against the U.S. dollar since March 2009, largely as a result of risk aversion brought on by the debt crisis in Europe.\par \par \uc2 The peso rallied on the news to 13.76 in late afternoon trade compared to 14.05 at the close Monday.\par \par \uc2 (This story and related background material will be available on The Wall Street Journal website, WSJ.com.)\par \par \uc2 Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has a reputation as a very conservative institution that tends to shy away from signaling a price for the peso. Rather, it tends to let the markets set the price.\par \par \uc2 But the European debt crisis has forced investors to seek safer investments like the U.S. dollar rather than \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the peso, which has tumbled from 11.75 per dollar on Aug. 1. A depreciating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  adds to inflationary pressure by making imports more expensive.\par \par \uc2 While the Bank of Mexico doesn't want to tell markets where it wants to see the peso and risk having the market test the bank's resolve, it also risked taking too hands off an approach, some economists say.\par \par \uc2 "Trying to draw a line in the sand is a dangerous move and the market will test you. But being quite so out of the market and laissez faire will also cause the markets to test you, too," said Gray Newman, chief economist for Latin America at \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 . "There are times you have to marry your economic principles with the way markets operate."\par \par \uc2 Newman said the move should help the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut interest rates in the coming months without having to worry about a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that is fast weakening.\par \par \uc2 For the past year and a half, the only mechanism the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  used to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the markets was a system of dollar put options that took advantage of peso appreciation to boost dollar reserves.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's foreign reserves stood at $139.6 billion at the end of last week, close to record levels. Mexico also has an unused $72 billion flexible credit line with the International Monetary Fund.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, the exchange commission said that it would suspend the sale of dollar put options for the time being. That suggests that the crisis has forced the bank's priorities to change, from making sure the peso doesn't get too strong and undermine the competitive position of Mexican exports to making sure the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't get too weak and stoke inflation.\par \par \uc2 The news also helped the price of Mexican government debt. The yield on benchmark 10-year bonds was down 15 basis points to 6.49%. Local bonds have been largely unscathed by the recent financial upheaval, with some investors hedging their risk through the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  instead.\par \par \uc2 The last time that the Bank of Mexico sold dollars in the exchange market was during the 2008-2009 global financial crisis. Between October 2008 and October 2009, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold more than $30 billion through direct auctions, conditional auctions with the 2% rule, and occasional unannounced direct \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curtail speculation. The conditional auctions were gradually lowered from $400 million to $250 million, and suspended in April 2010. [ 11-29-11 2019ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020111130e7bu0000j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
