\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-UPDATE 1-Mexico official sees market volatility\b0\par\par\uc2 637 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 November 2011\par 22:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Overall market liquidity has not been compromised\par \par \uc2 * Does not rule out action to support market functioning\par \par \uc2 (Adds details, quotes, background, byline)\par \par \uc2 By Luis Rojas and Krista Hughes\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, Nov 23 (Reuters) - Mexico's markets may be volatile in coming days but so far investors have shown no sign of abandoning peso investments, Deputy Finance Minister Gerardo Rodriguez said on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 In an interview with Reuters, Rodriguez said officials did not rule out taking necessary measures to stabilize markets, including \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 With U.S. financial markets closed on Thursday for a holiday, there may be more volatility on Mexican markets in coming days due to lower volumes.\par \par \uc2 "Markets become a bit more sensitive in periods of less liquidity. I think that's what we can expect in the next few days," Rodriguez said.\par \par \uc2 He said officials were not interested in defending a particular level of the peso <MXN=><MXN=D2>, which hit a 2-1/2-year low against the U.S. dollar on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 But asked about the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Rodriguez said: "At this moment I don't think it's possible to rule any option out, what I would say is that we are always closely monitoring developments in the markets.\par \par \uc2 "We see that market integrity has not been compromised, but this does not imply that we are not watching intently."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's last round of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was triggered in October 2008 when the peso dropped 6 percent in a single day and hit its lowest level in 16 years amid tight liquidity conditions.\par \par \uc2 So far, there are few signs foreign investors are turning against Mexico, Latin America's second-largest economy. Bond positions have remained firm and shorter-term debt has bounced back after a sell-off.\par \par \uc2 "The reduction was very, very slight and has been quickly recouped," Rodriguez said. "It's one factor that we have to keep watching closely to make sure that markets keep operating normally."\par \par \uc2 As of Nov. 11, the latest data available from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , foreign holdings of longer-term Mexican debt had dipped only 0.4 percent from a record high of 696.9 billion pesos ($49.2 billion) on Nov. 7.\par \par \uc2 NO WORSE THAN BEFORE\par \par \uc2 Rodriguez, who will play a key role in Mexico's presidency of the Group of 20 developed and emerging economies in 2012, said he did not see weakness in a bond sale in Germany on Wednesday as a sign of further deterioration in the euro zone economy.\par \par \uc2 "It's not about anything in particular, we are just seeing new manifestations of the same problems, which are sparking volatility," he said of the situation in Europe, where the euro <EUR=> hit a six-week low on Wednesday. [ID:nL5E7MN309]\par \par \uc2 The prospect of more International Monetary Fund help for the troubled euro zone was an issue to be addressed at the G20 level, he said.\par \par \uc2 IMF Managing Director Christine Lagarde will visit Mexico next week as part of a regional tour, but Rodriguez said he did not expect any changes to the country's credit line.\par \par \uc2 The IMF extended its credit line to Mexico to more than $70 billion the last time its chief visited Mexico, but Rodriguez said it was not time to review the agreement yet.\par \par \uc2 He confirmed that Mexico had not yet tapped the credit line, which runs until 2013, and said it remained a precautionary measure.\par \par \uc2 Officials were not considering any particular contingency plans in case of wider fallout from the euro zone crisis, but were monitoring conditions closely.\par \par \uc2 Economic activity remained solid and Mexican banks had sound capital buffers, Rodriguez said. ($1 = 14.1690)\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Michael O'Boyle and Ana Isabel Martinez; Editing by Leslie Adler)\par \par \uc2 MEXICO-MARKETS/ (UPDATE 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111126e7bn000ml\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
