\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Mexico peso hits 2-1/2 year low, real tumbles\b0\par\par\uc2 720 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 November 2011\par 15:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Latin American commodities fall as China factories slow\par \par \uc2 * Weak German debt auction spurs fears of wider EU crisis\par \par \uc2 * Brazil real slumps 2.9 pct, Mexico peso off 1.7 pct\par \par \uc2 (Recasts, adds comments)\par \par \uc2 By Michael O'Boyle and Jeb Blount\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY/SAO PAULO, Nov 23 (Reuters) - Mexico's peso slumped to a 2-1/2 year low on Wednesday and Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sank after weak Chinese factory data added to mounting concerns about Europe's debt troubles.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's real lost nearly 3 percent on concern a slowing Chinese economy will hit demand and prices for the country's iron ore, soybeans other commodity exports.\par \par \uc2 The weak Chinese data also weighed on Chile, as prices tumbled for copper, the country's top export.\par \par \uc2 Undermining demand for riskier assets around the world, a weak German bond sale sparked fears that Europe's debt crisis was beginning to threaten even Berlin. Leaders of France and Germany are still at odds over a longer-term structural solution. For details, see [ID:nL5E7MN1VS]\par \par \uc2 "Things are getting worse in Europe. The market has lost faith in the politicians coming up with a sound solution," said Clyde Wardle, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 HSBC\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 Major global banks, especially those based in Europe, are facing deepening losses on their euro zone bonds. Their units in Latin America will likely cut back on loans to the region, which would drag down local growth, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's peso <MXN=><MXN=D2> slumped 1.72 percent to 14.23 per dollar and hit its weakest level since April 2009.\par \par \uc2 The peso's sharp losses have raised concerns that \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt losses.\par \par \uc2 Mexican Deputy Finance Minister Gerardo Rodriguez told Reuters that officials were closely monitoring markets, but he said recent volatility had not compromised the integrity of the market for Mexican assets. [ID:nWNA4350]\par \par \uc2 Rodriguez also said officials were not interested in defending a particular level for the peso.\par \par \uc2 Mexico has the only freely traded \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Latin America and analysts doubt authorities will follow Brazil's recent moves to defend its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Moreover, the peso's weakness will help boost earnings from exports and make them more competitive abroad.\par \par \uc2 "Given the highly uncertain external environment, we believe authorities will be comfortable with a weak peso, which is helping the export sector," Wardle said.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso's slump back past the psychological 14-per-dollar level this week has pushed investors to call off bets that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could cut interest rates on Dec. 2.\par \par \uc2 A Reuters poll showed 19 of 19 economists surveyed see Mexico holding its benchmark rate steady at 4.50 percent. [ID:nWNA4305]\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is probably unwilling to cut interest rates when a prolonged bout of weakness in the peso since August could end up feeding into higher inflation through more expensive imports, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 "What is worrying the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is what is happening with the exchange rate. If it stays around 14, or above 14, there will be pressure on inflation," said Salvador Orozco, a debt strategist at Santander in Mexico City.\par \par \uc2 In Brazil, the real <BRBY> weakened 2.85 percent to 1.8578 to the dollar, its weakest close since Oct. 4 <BRLCLOSE=>.\par \par \uc2 The real has lost nearly 9 percent this month, putting it in a virtual tie with the New Zealand dollar <NZD=> as the biggest loser among the more than 150 \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  tracked by Thomson Reuters against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Data on Wednesday showed factory activity in China shrank at its fastest pace in 32 months. [ID:nL4E7MN1HM]\par \par \uc2 China, the world's second-largest national economy, is Brazil's top trading partner.\par \par \uc2 "The data is showing a major contraction in China, which means that in addition to export volumes, prices are falling, too," said Luciano Rostagno, chief strategist with the Sao Paulo unit of \uc2 WestLB\uc2 , a German bank.\par \par \uc2 Chile's peso <CLP=CL> bid 1.2 percent weaker at 524.70, its weakest in nearly seven weeks as copper prices hit a one-month low. [ID:nL5E7MN0R3]\par \par \uc2 Chile gets about 55 percent of its export earnings from copper.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Michael O'Boyle; Editing by Dan Grebler)\par \par \uc2 CN-MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111126e7bn000e6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
