\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Mexican peso approaches two year low\b0\par\par\uc2 717 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 November 2011\par 20:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Peso weaker than 14 boosts outlook for c.bank action\par \par \uc2 * Peso erases losses to gain against U.S. dollar\par \par \uc2 * Weak Mexico peso, strong GDP raises inflation concern\par \par \uc2 * Latin America \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  trades mixed on Europe debt, U.S. GDP\par \par \uc2 (Updates prices to close)\par \par \uc2 By Jeb Blount and Michael O'Boyle\par \par \uc2 RIO DE JANEIRO/MEXICO CITY, Nov 22 (Reuters) - Investors eyed the potential for \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and called off bets of an imminent interest-rate cut after Mexico's peso nearly hit its weakest point in two years.\par \par \uc2 The peso <MXN=D2> firmed 0.25 percent to 13.9850. Earlier it slipped 0.52 percent to 14.0928, just short of 14.1396, a September intraday price that is the weakest since April 2009.\par \par \uc2 A weaker peso, which has lost about 12 percent in the last three months during the European debt and banking crisis, makes imports more expensive and risks driving inflation higher, a result the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is charged with preventing.\par \par \uc2 "To short the peso beyond 14 right now you have to be very brave," said Alberto Bernal, head of research at Bulltick Capital Markets in Miami. "If the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  comes in to protect the peso, they (investors) will get hammered."\par \par \uc2 As the peso has declined over the last three months - it is the seventh-worst performer among the 36 most-traded \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar in the period - concern about inflation and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has risen.\par \par \uc2 That in turn has seen investors increase their bets that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not cut interest rates at its next meeting on Dec. 2.\par \par \uc2 Inflation concerns rose after a report on Tuesday showed the Mexican economy <MXGDPQ=ECI> grew a seasonally adjusted 1.3 percent in the third quarter, nearly two-thirds higher than the 0.8 percent estimate of 16 economists surveyed by Reuters. [ID:nN1E7AL0J3] and [ID:nN1E7AF22O]\par \par \uc2 Some said expectations of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  are overdone. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  unless the world financial system seizes up, said Benito Berber, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst with Japan's Nomura Securities in New York.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the peso in 2008 and 2009 as bank lending and financial system liquidity dried up after the U.S. mortgage and banking crisis.\par \par \uc2 "The bank will only \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if there is no liquidity in the markets," Berber said.\par \par \uc2 Just such a situation, though, may be in the offing as investors seek protection from a potential European financial collapse by buying the dollar in favor of the peso and other Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which are considered riskier.\par \par \uc2 "If Europe falls apart, if the euro breaks up, the world financial system will collapse and there is nothing you can do to hedge your risk," Bernal said. "We are hostage to that situation in Mexico."\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere in Latin America, \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded mixed against the U.S. dollar as Europe's debt crisis worsened, the U.S. economy showed signs of life and investors moved to protect holdings ahead of a long holiday weekend in the United States.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's real <BRBY> gained 0.343 percent to 1.8058 after weakening as much as 0.88 percent. Chile's peso <CLP=CL> was unchanged from Monday at 518.40.\par \par \uc2 "This is a consolidation day, a day where people are closing up positions ahead of the Thanksgiving weekend and very little solid news," said Kathy Lien, a strategist with GFT \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  a New York \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  brokerage. "We're not going to get a trend, until things in Europe settle down."\par \par \uc2 The region's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuated as Spanish bond yields, a proxy for borrowing costs, rose to a 14-year high, raising concern that Spain may soon join Italy and Greece as countries facing unstable finances and rising default risk.\par \par \uc2 The United States, the world's largest economy, cut its estimate for third-quarter growth to 2 percent from 2.5 percent. The decline, though, was based on inventories, one of the most volatile parts of GDP figures. The data suggests that fourth-quarter growth could be greater than 3 percent, the highest in 18 months. [ID:nN1E7AL0E2]\par \par \uc2 "The GDP cut is not a sign of slowing," Lien said.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Andrew Hay)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111125e7bm001a0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
