\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Peso's Plunge Tests Bank Of Mexico Resolve -RBS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,319 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 November 2011\par 17:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 11:14 (Dow Jones) The plunging Mexican peso, which has lost 7.5% against the US dollar since end-October, is testing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  resolve to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, RBS analysts say in a note. "Without an improvement in risk and lower exchange rate in the short-term, the probability of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  increases to counter the risks of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  pass-through to inflation and a disorderly unwind of foreign holdings in the stock of fixed income," firm says. And Bank of Mexico's reluctance to act comes partly from implications of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  freely traded offshore, which reduces the effectiveness of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Peso now recently at MXN13.9910 in \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  City, according to Infosel, vs MXN14.08 at the open and MXN13.7280 at Friday's close. (juan.montes@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:13 (Dow Jones) In yet another datapoint highlighting Apple's (AAPL) sharp growth, market researcher Canalys expects the Mac maker to become the world's biggest PC maker by shipments in 1H. AAPL can thank the iPad, which Canalys counts as a PC. The popular tablet has expanded the company's PC market share to 15% from 9% the past year, says Canalys. (ian.sherr@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.canalys.com/newsroom/apple-track-become-leading-global-pc-vendor"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.canalys.com/newsroom/apple-track-become-leading-global-pc-vendor}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 11:11 (Dow Jones) US stocks are lower in morning trade as investors eye another day of stress for Spanish bond yields and weaker-than-expected reading on US 3Q GDP. Losses lately are starting to add up. Dow industrials now down 5.6% since Nov. 11. DJIA, S&P 500 and Nasdaq each finished at lowest level in a month or more Monday. DJIA sheds 66, or 0.6%, to 11481 recently. (brendan.conway@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:05 (Dow Jones) Investors are avoiding any commercial paper tainted with euro-zone debt, specially debt from countries whose names are in the headlines repeatedly, says trader at primary dealer. "Swiss, Canadian, Japanese and U.S. companies are able to sell paper for a little over just overnight but otherwise, buyers are sticking with very short paper," he said. "Confidence is quite weak." Flows are light this week and will likely be light next week as well, he said, with some pick-up in early December for those who want to get over the year's end. (anusha.shrivastava@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:58 (Dow Jones) Small caps have underperformed broader stock indexes this year, but when it comes to banking, the reverse is true. The S&P Small Cap 600 Financial sector is down about 9% this year, well outpacing broader benchmarks like the \uc2 Financial Select Sector SPDR\uc2  (XLF); it's off 24%. "It would appear that in recent weeks investors have started to hone in on the specific problems facing large, highly regulated financial firms in the currently inhospitable environment," writes Oscar Gruss CEO Michael Shaoul. "For the smaller banking franchises, the environment can be said to be challenging, but there is a sense that the worst of the legacy issues from the collapse of real estate markets are now behind us." (brendan.conway@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:57 (Dow Jones) Two more analysts join \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  in chopping Jefferies (JEF) F4Q EPS view, citing weak operating environment. JMP Securities slashes 4Q EPS forecast to 11c from 27c, saying JEF will take a hit from capital raising efforts that were worse-than-expected. JMP expects core revenue to rise 11% sequentially to $524M, but says equity capital markets activity will plunge 90%, while bond underwriting should drop by one-half. Sandler O'Neill cuts JEF operating EPS outlook to 14c from 26c; says it's comfortable with JEF's balance sheet despite market concerns, and share-price plunge has eliminated JEF's historic valuation premium to large-cap peers. JEF off 0.7% at $10.13. (brett.philbin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:55 (Dow Jones) Treasury investors could get the first sub-1% yield at today's 5-year Treasury auction. The yield on previously issued 5-years is at 0.94%, compared with the record-low auction yield of 1.02% in September. The meager amount could be a problem as yield-hungry investors may stand pat. But given the euro-zone crisis, demand is still robust for Treasurys as a safe harbor--as Monday's 2-year sale showed. Joseph Leary at Citi says the sale can go either way. "If we look at the last time we had the lowest yield, in September, the auction actually came in better than expected." (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:55 (Dow Jones) US equity correlations have reached historical highs as macro factors are impacting returns more than either industry- or stock-specific factors, John Linehan, director of US equities at T Rowe Price, said Tuesday. That offers significant opportunity for those willing to do fundamental research on companies, he said. He sees opportunity in technology and select cyclicals and said the emergence of the aspirant emerging-market consumer is an investment theme that will play out for years. (daisy.maxey@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:51 (Dow Jones) Colombian stocks are down for a sixth straight session. Global markets are partly to blame but also at fault is \uc2 Ecopetrol\uc2 , the country's largest oil company and one of the heaviest-weighted shares in the index. State-controlled firm announced 2012 investment plan overnight and shares recently down 0.3% today at COP3,945, marking a fifth straight day of losses. Company sees the pace of growth in crude production slowing, to an 11% increase in 2012 y/y. That, analysts at InterBolsa say, compares with an 18% increase in each of the years 2010 and 2011. (dan.molinski@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:48 (Dow Jones) \uc2 Dollar Tree\uc2  (DLTR) is sending a message with its new $400M share repurchase program, which represents close to 4M shares at yesterday's close of $75.69. The announcement is DLTR's third of its kind in three months and "a strong indicator of management's confidence with current business trends, as well as it's comfort on gross margins, which we believe are likely to improve throughout fiscal 2012," \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  says. DLTR up 2.6% to $77.67. (karen.talley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:41 (Dow Jones) Gold and copper hold their gains even as equities shuffle indecisively on a weaker-than-expected US GDP reading and tremors in the EU bond market. Both metals slid to 4-week lows Monday amid stocks' latest slide. "Some market players clearly view the price slump as exaggerated and are taking advantage of the low price levels to snatch bargains," says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 . Few are betting on a sustained rally, though, as metals markets continue to be held captive by the latest view on whether a euro-zone credit crunch is imminent. Comex December gold and copper are both up 1.4%. (matt.day@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:39 (Dow Jones) Longbow moves to the sidelines on \uc2 A. Schulman\uc2  (SHLM) and \uc2 PolyOne\uc2  (POL) as plastic distributors have been "significantly impacted by inventory destocking, particularly in Europe." Falling resin prices have triggered inventory correction in the channel, firm says, while increased price competition and softer demand in October and November forced distributors to adjust prices more rapidly despite higher-priced inventories. Furthermore, a softer December is around the corner and macroeconomic uncertainties still linger, causing Longbow to also lower its 2012 EPS estimates on both companies. POL down 3.8% to $10.29, SHLM down 4.3% to $20.03. (ian.thomson@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Equities Market Talk Editors, \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Paul Vigna, 212 416-2184,paul.vigna@dowjones.com \par Visit the Market Talk website at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.djnmarkettalk.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.djnmarkettalk.com}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 11-22-11 1114ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4035   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020111122e7bm000cn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
