\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: October's CPI Rise Start Of A Trend -Banamex\b0\par\par\uc2 1,426 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 November 2011\par 19:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 1:11 (Dow Jones) Last month's 0.67% rise in Mexico's CPI marks the beginning of an upward trend in inflation, says Citi's Banamex. The increase, largely due to the end of summer subsidies for energy consumption, pushed CPI up 3.2% from a year earlier. Meanwhile, Banamex says lower prices for fresh produce and weak consumer demand appear to be counteracting the peso's depreciation against the dollar since August. It was recently at MXN13.531, compared with MXN11.738 at the end of July. (amy.guthrie@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1:07 (Dow Jones) The US government continues to cash in on fears about the euro zone. When Italy is watching nervously with a 10-year yield surging above 7%, the US Treasury just sold $24 bln benchmark 10-year notes at a meager yield of 2.03%. Still, the yield is higher than 2.016% right before the sale as the rally that pushes yields lower cut some buying interest. The bid to cover, a gauge of overall demand, is 2.64%, the lowest since December 2009 and compared to 3.12 from the past eight sales. The indirect bid -- a proxy measure of foreign demand -- is still robust at 41.6%, compared to 44.3% for the past eight sales. The direct bid is 8.2%, compared to 11.5% for the past eight auctions. (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1:06 (Dow Jones) It may be time to take a breather from \uc2 Macy's\uc2  (M) shares following retailer's mixed 3Q results, \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  says. "The breakdown of margins in the quarter and 4Q guidance below street expectations will likely disappoint investors near-term, with the primary question mark being the sustainability of the credit (card) tailwind in 2012 and beyond," firm says. "While we continue to believe M represents among the best longer-term core holdings in the group with top-line, margin, and capital allocation drivers in place, we would let the dust settle given the (stock's) strong recent run before aggressively adding to positions." M falls 3.8% to $30.94, but are still up 22% over the last three months. (karen.talley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1:05 (Dow Jones) Brazil's increase in minimum wages next year -- determined by a formula incorporating past inflation and GDP growth -- will likely be close to 14%, translating into BRL22 billion of additional government spending on things such as social security benefits, \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  says. With expanded spending next year and revenue estimates that are "too optimistic," the government will likely have trouble meeting its primary surplus target in 2012, BofA's David Beker writes. (paulo.winterstein@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1:03 (Dow Jones) Analysts dial down their views on Alaska Communications (ALSK) in the wake of its surprise 3Q loss. RBC downgrades to underperform and halves its price target to $4, saying a 2012 dividend cut amid an expected loss of high-margin regulatory revenue. S&P \uc2 Capital IQ\uc2  goes ALSK to sell and slashes its 12-month target to $2.50 from $5.50, saying the company's 4G spending focus could be another driver behind potentially lower dividends. ALSK tumbles 24% to $5.61 and earlier hit a 2 1/2-year low. (ian.thomson@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:55 (Dow Jones) The latest data from ICI show there's still no new appetite for US stock mutual funds, with outflows for the latest week remaining in line with activity the past month and a half. There's been net outflows from the funds for 11 straight weeks, according to the fund-industry group, with the week ended Nov. 2's $3.36B in line with activity since mid-September. October's stock rebound didn't persuade fresh money to come into equities--which didn't happen either on a consistent basis as stocks soared from the March 2009 bottom. So have investors washed their hands for good in putting new cash into stock? Or do they have no fresh capital to put to work, regardless of the asset class? (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:49 (Dow Jones) News of Carlyle cutting management fee of new $2.3B real estate fund isn't surprising. Already large institutions are using their size to negotiate better terms, so it's a matter of time when other investors also press for them. It may have be a temporary dent on Carlyle's clout, especially ahead of it IPO, but the large buyout firm will manage through by gathering more assets. The real question lies in what this means for small-to-mid sized private equity firms, which don't have the scale to offer as attractive fees as the 0.75% that Carlyle is offering. Consolidation seems inevitable. (amy.or@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:48 (Dow Jones) A trial comparing a type of heart-valve implantation to surgical valve replacement in lower-risk patients was terminated because of "excess events" in patients treated with the implanted valve, according to an abstract of the study. That could put pressure on \uc2 Edwards Lifesciences\uc2 ' (EW) stock, says \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2 . EW is trying to get the method approved in US, and despite the trial cancellation, the bank still expects the procedure to be approved next year. It accounts for about half of company's sales of its Sapien device in Europe. More details are expected during a medical meeting Thursday. EW is down 3.5% at $73.61, more than the broader market's swoon. (anjali.athavaley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:47 (Dow Jones) Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  should \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if the Mexican peso depreciates further against the US dollar, says \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  analyst Alberto Ramos. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be justified to add another instrument to the toolkit: \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to anchor a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that is now trading weaker to fair value." Since the end of 2010, the peso has lost around 9.3% of its value vs USD, mainly due to external factors such as the European debt crisis. In Mexico City, peso recently at MXN13.5120, according to Infosel, vs MXN13.336 at yesterday's close. (juan.montes@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:43 (Dow Jones) Anadarko (APC) is outperforming broader stocks to the downside as positive results from a Brazilian deepwater well are more than offset by partner Tullow's (TWL.LN, TUWOY) failure at finding a commercial discovery in a well off Liberia. Tudor Pickering says the closely watched Montserrado well could have been "huge," so the news a big disappointment, especially when combined with production issues at Jubilee, where Tullow and APC are also partners. In the other hand, the Brazil results could increase the value of assets there, which are for sale. APC is down 5.3% at $79.49 while TUWOY is down 8.4% at $10.75. (angel.gonzalez@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:40 (Dow Jones) \uc2 Pacific Rubiales\uc2  Energy (PRE.T), a Toronto-based oil firm and Colombia's top independent crude producer, declines 2.5% to COP39,660. The firm posted a 71% increase in 3Q net to $193.7 million on higher crude prices and output, but the production was partly hobbled by a temporary halt in production at its largest oil field in Colombia due to labor protests. Despite showing some disappointment with the results, brokerage firm \uc2 InterBolsa SA\uc2  says that \uc2 Pacific Rubiales\uc2  "has one of the best set of fundamentals among the oil and gas companies operating in Colombia." (darcy.crowe@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:38 (Dow Jones) After listening to the 3Q conference calls of Blackstone (BX), \uc2 KKR\uc2  and Apollo (APO), one comes away with the impression that the underlying portfolio companies' \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  are solid and valuations are looking much better with October's equities rebound. But private-equity participants at the 2011 Leadership in the Private Capital Markets conference believe industry woes including lower returns are here to stay on dry-powder overhang, quality assets commanding high sales prices and less and unpredictable leverage availability. \uc2 Bingham McCutchen\uc2 's \uc2 Benjamin Burkhart\uc2  says asset sellers are still reluctant to adjust their price expectations, but will need to when "2012 earnings expectations crystallize a bit more." (amy.or@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Equities Market Talk Editors, \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Paul Vigna, 212 416-2184,paul.vigna@dowjones.com \par Visit the Market Talk website at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.djnmarkettalk.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.djnmarkettalk.com}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 11-09-11 1311ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4035   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020111109e7b9000jj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
