\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Mexico bondholders resist sell-off, shield economy\b0\par\par\uc2 833 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2011\par 23:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 * Mexican bondholders sit tight amid global panic\par \par \uc2 * More long-term investors seen, little hot money\par \par \uc2 * If bonds sell off, could trigger cenbank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 By Michael O'Boyle\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 MEXICO\b0 \highlight\uc2  CITY, Oct 14 (Reuters) - Global investors in Mexican bonds sat tight through a brutal September for riskier assets, indicating a new resiliency among investors that will lend stability to Mexico's economy.\par \par \uc2 The amount of peso debt held by foreign investors tripled over the last two years to a record high of $70 billion last month as investors starved by ultra-low U.S. interest rates piled into emerging market debt.\par \par \uc2 Those huge inflows made the Mexican bond market look like a bubble ready to pop when volatility hit last month, but investors surprisingly stuck to their positions despite \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  losses.\par \par \uc2 A sudden reversal of those flows could spur huge \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  losses similar to 2008, when Mexico's peso tumbled by the most since the devastating 1994 Tequila Crisis.\par \par \uc2 "The magnitude of the foreign position is frightening," said Gabriel Casillas, an economist at \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  in Mexico City. "But the investor base is broader. It is more long-term investors and less hot money."\par \par \uc2 When Mexico's peso <MXN=> slumped about 13 percent against the dollar in September to its weakest level since 2009, short-term foreign investors dumped nearly $3.3 billion in Mexican T-bills, or one-fifth of their holdings, according to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data.\par \par \uc2 But investors in longer-term notes sold only about $760 million, or 1.5 percent of holdings, and the notes still hover near a record high.\par \par \uc2 ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\par \par \uc2 Mexican bond time bomb? {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/hyq93s"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/hyq93s }}}\par \par \uc2 Foreigners sit tight: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/raq44s"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/raq44s }}}\par \par \uc2 ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\par \par \uc2 Had more bond investors sold, it would have deepened \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  losses, which could trigger more bond sales, further weakening the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a vicious cycle.\par \par \uc2 A sell-off in bonds would push up market rates, dragging on growth, while a weaker peso could stoke inflation and make it harder for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut benchmark credit costs.\par \par \uc2 READY TO RALLY?\par \par \uc2 Mexico, with Latin America's most freely traded \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bonds, has been a magnet for investors fleeing crippled public finances and rock-bottom rates in major economies.\par \par \uc2 The recent sell-off in the peso tested the mettle of these new investors, and the resiliency of bond positions suggests they will not be spooked by another round of peso weakness.\par \par \uc2 "This has been a good, solid test," said Enrique Alvarez, a debt and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at IDEAglobal in New York.\par \par \uc2 If European leaders make good on a pledge to deliver a bold plan to contain the continent's debt crisis by the end of the month, "there could be a pretty large flow coming back into Mexico, Brazil and emerging markets," Alvarez said.\par \par \uc2 Data from fund-tracker EPFR shows that redemptions from local-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  EM bond funds scaled back to a relative trickle this week after spiking in September.\par \par \uc2 Fund managers stocked up on cash to prepare for even more redemptions, and now they may be ready to start buying bonds again, RBS Securities wrote in a note. A rally back by Mexican bonds has already begun.\par \par \uc2 The yield on Mexico's benchmark 10-year peso bond <MX10YT=RR> fell on Friday below its 50-day moving average, down more than 60 basis points since last week to 6.17 percent, suggesting yields could drop even more.\par \par \uc2 Moreover, the peso's tumble last month shook out speculators. Net futures contracts on the Chicago exchange wagering on a stronger peso plummeted since July, while bets on a weaker peso are hovering near a four-year high.\par \par \uc2 That means there would now be less of a scramble to dump the peso if global sentiment sours again.\par \par \uc2 "The risk of another violent sell-off in the peso is more contained since positioning has improved," said Roberto Melzi, a strategist at \uc2 Barclays\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Edwin Gutierrez, who helps manage $7 billion of emerging market debt at \uc2 Aberdeen Asset Management\uc2  in London, expects volatility to keep the peso on the ropes but doubts there will be a repeat of 2008's market slump.\par \par \uc2 "Emerging markets came back pretty quickly then, and we will come back even better than developed markets this time," he said.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's policy makers, perhaps the most orthodox supporters of free markets in Latin America, have so far refrained from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt the peso's precipitous slide.\par \par \uc2 But a sudden bond outflow could push Mexico to tap nearly $210 billion from a war chest of record \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and a credit line with the International Monetary Fund.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  will have to show their hand in terms of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if they are tested again," said Siobhan Morden, head of Latin America debt strategy at RBS Securities in Stamford, Connecticut. "There is no way you could adjust to that shock of a mass exodus."\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Michael O'Boyle; Editing by Leslie Adler)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-MEXICO/BONDS (ANALYSIS)  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111017e7ae001lw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
