\par\par \b \uc2 Bank Of Mexico Governor Sees Stronger Peso In Medium Term\b0\par\par\uc2 327 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Oktober 2011\par 18:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY (Dow Jones)--Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Agustin Carstens said Monday that the Mexican peso should strengthen in the medium-term, anchored by the country's "strong economic fundamentals."\par \par \uc2 Concerns about the global economy pushed the peso to a 2 1/2-year low in September of MXN14 to the U.S. dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was trading around MXN13.8865 early Monday, off 17% from its strongest 2011 close, reached in April.\par \par \uc2 Mexico adopted a free-floating peso in 1994, which Carstens said has helped infuse the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market with stability.\par \par \uc2 "The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  stopped \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to maintain a certain exchange rate many years ago," Carstens said.\par \par \uc2 When the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  opts to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Carstens emphasized, it does so in a transparent manner so that market participants are not caught off guard.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Mexico sold dollars in the market under several different mechanisms between October 2008 and October 2009 as a lack of liquidity in the market put pressure on the exchange rate. This time officials say there has been adequate liquidity, and have shown no signs they are planning to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In contrast to the flight to safety behind the peso's recent sharp depreciation, Carstens highlighted that the average maturity for Mexican government debt in pesos, at 7.4 years, now exceeds that of the U.S., at 5.2 years.\par \par \uc2 The willingness of investors to extend Mexico such long debt maturities is a reflection of confidence, Carstens said, reiterating the country's commitment to stable fiscal and macroeconomic policies.\par \par \uc2 Going forward, Carstens said that world economic policymakers require a clear and immediate response from the largest economies to the serious problems of indebtedness plaguing the U.S. and Europe, including a more committed strategy for the adjustment of their fiscal accounts.\par \par \uc2 -By Amy Guthrie, Dow Jones Newswires; (5255) 5980-5177; amy.guthrie@dowjones.com [ 03-10-11 1615GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020111003e7a300142\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
