\par\par \b \uc2 Mexican Peso Ends Weaker As Risk Rout Hits Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 570 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2011\par 22:23\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Peso loses ground for third straight session\par \par \uc2 --Bank of Mexico sells $600 million in dollar put options\par \par \uc2 --Eventual \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains a discussion point\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Anthony Harrup \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MEXICO CITY (Dow Jones)--Mexico's peso closed weaker against the U.S. dollar for a third consecutive session Friday, losing ground along with local stocks and bonds in another bout of global risk selling.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The peso was quoted closing in Mexico City at MXN13.8735 to the dollar, according to Infosel, compared with MXN13.69 Thursday, for a loss on the week, month and quarter.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investor worries about euro zone debt troubles, and a slowdown in U.S. and global economic growth, have buffeted the peso the past two months. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is off 17% from its strongest close of the year in April, and near to its weakest level in 2 1/2 years.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Friday's U.S. economic data were mixed, with better-than-expected consumer confidence and Chicago factory readings offsetting a surprise dip in personal income, but participants appeared determined to focus on the negative such as a drop in German retail sales and a rise in euro-zone inflation that could keep the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from cutting interest rates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On the local front, the Bank of Mexico sold all $600 million of its monthly dollar put options for October, which will allow holders to sell dollars to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if the peso appreciates next month. While demand for the options fell to $1.55 billion from $2.5 billion a month ago, the average premium rose to MXN65.32 per $1,000 from MXN61.72. Someone tested a bid for MXN1.01 per $1,000 for $10 million in options.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The September options expired without any being exercised as the peso depreciated for a second straight month.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With the continuing volatility in exchange markets, and worries about developed economies hitting emerging market assets, analysts are discussing the likelihood of eventual \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Mexico\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of America\f28 \uc2 \f2 Merrill Lynch\f28 \uc2 \f2  said it thinks Mexico and Chile are the two major Latin American countries least likely to see \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , "unless there is a fast and disorderly move in those \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mexico's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  commission, made up of Finance Ministry and Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Mexico\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  officials, is unlikely to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the near term since officials see the peso weakness overdone as a result of risk aversion, and expect the peso to recover soon.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "In the recent past, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have only occurred when the market turned highly illiquid. And in that case, they were implemented in a transparent manner, with clear rules. Therefore, we do not expect any change in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  process in coming weeks in our central scenario," BofA said in a report.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Local stocks fell 0.6%, well off intraday lows, and government bonds lost ground, pushing yields higher. The yield on benchmark 10-year bonds rose 10 basis points to 6.58%, and 20-year yields were up 6 basis points to 7.58%. The local debt market has seen only modest outflows despite the peso's weakness.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "In Mexico, positioning risk in fixed income is probably still quite high," RBS said in a report.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Anthony Harrup, Dow Jones Newswires; (5255) 5980-5176, anthony.harrup@dowjones.com [ 30-09-11 2023GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1027 1852   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110930e79u00187\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
