\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Brazil, Mexico  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  rebound on euro-zone hopes\b0\par\par\uc2 659 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2011\par 01:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  (Refiles with fresh headline)\par \par \uc2  * Brazil real, Mexico peso gain in day of see-saw trading\par \par \uc2  * \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rebound on expectation Europe to act on debt\par \par \uc2  * Chile peso firms vs U.S. dollar; Colombia peso weakens\par \par \uc2  * Peru's sol trades unchanged from Friday on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  By Jeb Blount\par \par \uc2  RIO DE JANEIRO, Sept 26 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso and Brazil's real erased losses and gained against the U.S. dollar on Monday, on expectations that Europe will find a way to cut Greek debt and bolster its banks, preventing sovereign debt defaults from choking off global lending and growth.\par \par \uc2  Euro-zone officials are seeking new ways to boost their means to fight a widening debt crisis, Lorenzo Bini Smaghi, an executive board member of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said on Monday in New York. [ID:nLDE78P01H]\par \par \uc2  The expectation that the euro zone and the ECB -- after months of ineffectual action -- will take stronger measures to prevent a Greek default from spreading to Portugal, Ireland, Spain and Italy helped markets rebound in late trading on Monday.\par \par \uc2  "The market seems to have convinced itself that the Europeans will take stronger action," said Pedro Tuesta, Latin America \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and fixed-income analyst with 4Cast Inc. in Washington.\par \par \uc2 "I don't buy it, but a lot of people seem to, and they're the ones who've bid the market back up today," he added. "That's about the only way to explain today's gains. Nothing really fundamental has changed, Greece is still a mess and whatever happens will be painful."\par \par \uc2  Brazil's real <BRBY> gained 0.88 percent to 1.8200 reais to the dollar. Earlier, it weakened as much as 1.55 percent and gained as much as 1.32 percent.\par \par \uc2  Mexico's peso <MXN=D2> firmed 0.60 percent to 13.5019 to the dollar. Earlier, it firmed as much as 1.35 percent and weakened as much as 1.44 percent.\par \par \uc2  HURT BY WEAK U.S. HOME SALES\par \par \uc2  Early declines were fueled on Monday by concern about growth in the United States, which is responsible for nearly a quarter of world economic output and the source of about 80 percent of Mexican export earnings.\par \par \uc2  Weak U.S. housing added to concern that the world's largest economy could slip back into recession.\par \par \uc2  New single-family home sales fell in August to a six-month low, a sign the country's near-zero interest rates and expanded credit haven't been enough to boost consumer appetite for long-term investments. [ID:nS1E78P0CJ].\par \par \uc2  In such situations, many global investors sell assets in Latin America and other markets considered risky, and buy assets denominated in dollars, the world's most negotiable, or liquid, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  Weak U.S. growth and concern about European debt are the main causes for the Brazilian real's and Mexican peso's declines this month, the biggest in Latin America and among the worst in the world.\par \par \uc2  Despite today's gains, the Mexican peso has still lost nearly 10 percent in September and the Brazilian real nearly 13 percent.\par \par \uc2  Any doubt about Europe's ability or willingness to solve its debt problems is likely to start the slide again, Tuesta said.\par \par \uc2  Chile's peso gained for a second day, firming 0.78 percent to 512.00.\par \par \uc2  Colombia's peso <COP2=STFX> weakened 0.16 percent to 1,907.00 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2  Peru's sol <PEN=PE> closed trading unchanged from Friday at 2.7700 to the dollar after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold $186 million in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and sold 100 million soles ($36 million) of dollar-linked Adjustable Certificates of Deposit (CDRs).\par \par \uc2  The sale of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and notes drives up demand for soles, putting pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to gain or limiting losses.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Michael O'Boyle in Mexico City; Editing by Andrew Hay and Jan Paschal)    \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (REFILE)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110929e79q001n6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
