\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Brazil real hits 12-yr high, minister jawbones\b0\par\par\uc2 792 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juli 2011\par 17:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Investors test Brazil's resolve to limit gains\par \par \uc2 * Finance minister threatens more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Brazil real firms 0.7 pct, \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  peso off 0.1 pct\par \par \uc2 By Michael O'Boyle\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, July 25 (Reuters) - Brazil's real hit a more than 12-year high on Monday as investors tested the resolve of Brazilian authorities worried by its gains, while fears about the U.S. debt stalemate held back other Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Brazil's real broke past the 1.55 level to trade at its strongest since the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was freely floated in early 1999.\par \par \uc2 Local authorities have tried to undermine bets on the real with a range of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures, from dollar purchases to taxes. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength is hurting local industries by cutting into export profits and making rival imports cheaper.\par \par \uc2 But the market is betting that authorities may allow further gains in the real to help keep import prices down and cool inflation, analysts said. Inflation expectations for 2012 crept up in a poll released on Monday. [ID:nN1E76O03S]\par \par \uc2 Brazil's Finance Minister Guido Mantega on Monday scoffed at the idea that the country uses its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fight inflation and said the government stood ready to take further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures. [ID:nN1E76O18G]\par \par \uc2 But analysts doubt authorities are willing to turn to more draconian measures, such as higher taxes on stock market investments, that could spur sharp losses in the real and end up further fanning price pressures.\par \par \uc2 "There is very little the government can do unless they turn to less market-friendly measures," said Marianna Costa, chief economist with Link Investimentos, a Sao Paulo brokerage.\par \par \uc2 The real <BRBY> bid 0.71 percent stronger to close at 1.5427 per dollar. Some analysts expect the real to hit 1.50 per dollar this year.\par \par \uc2 RATE ALLURE\par \par \uc2 Costa said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could step up its dollar purchases in the spot and futures markets, but that would do little more than slow a further appreciation of the real.\par \par \uc2 Last week, Brazil tightened borrowing costs for the fifth time this year to raise its benchmark rate to 12.50 percent, the highest of any major economy <0#PRIME=>.\par \par \uc2 The allure of Brazilian interest rates has spurred a tide of money flows into Latin America's top economy. Signs that slowing global growth will keep the United States, Europe and Japan rates down for the foreseeable future will fuel further demand for Brazilian assets, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 "With those rate hikes postponed, this only insures that the flows to Brazil could continue at the same pace," Costa said.\par \par \uc2 Moreover, continued concerns that the U.S. debt rating could be downgraded should weigh on the dollar, thereby supporting commodity prices and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  linked to raw materials exports, such as the real.\par \par \uc2 A divided U.S. Congress on Monday pursued rival budget plans that appeared unlikely to win broad support, pushing the country closer to a debt default. Even if the debt limit is raised, the country may face a ratings downgrade if lawmakers cannot find a way to cut the deficit. [ID:nN1E76M0B0]\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Mexico and Colombia, the two Latin American economies with the closest trade ties to the United States, saw volatile trading. But their pesos pared losses as the market held to bets that politicians would manage to avert a U.S. default.\par \par \uc2 "I think everyone still has this hope that they will do something. They are playing politics as politicians do, but in the end, it will get done," said Ramses Villela, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at Bulltick Capital Markets in Mexico City.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's peso <MXN=> slipped 0.11 to 11.6430 per dollar, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  erased steep losses that had pushed the cost of dollars in pesos up to just below its 50-day moving average at 11.7250.\par \par \uc2 Colombia's peso <COP2=STFX> bid 0.44 percent weaker at 1,763.50 per dollar, but it recovered from an earlier 1.5 percent loss.\par \par \uc2 In a recent poll of analysts, all but one of the 25 analysts surveyed expected that Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will raise its benchmark interest rate by 25 basis points to 4.5 percent at its Friday meeting, marking the sixth straight rate increase since February. [ID:nN1E76O172]\par \par \uc2 Chile's peso <CLP=CL> bid 0.24 percent weaker at 462.40 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Chile and Colombia remain near three-year highs and further strength could push their authorities to try further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Silvio Cascione in Sao Paulo, Jean Luis Arce in Mexico City and Nelson Bocanegra in Bogota)\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Theodore d'Afflisio)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2   (UPDATE 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110728e77p001mz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal