\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Bank Of Mexico Policy Could Tighten Without Rate Increase\b0\par\par\uc2 633 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juli 2011\par 19:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global FX & Fixed Income News\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 --Because of easing inflationary pressures, the Bank of Mexico may gradually see tighter monetary policy without hiking interest rates, says deputy governor\par \par \uc2 --The Mexican peso's strength helps curb inflationary pressures\par \par \uc2 --The euro-zone debt crisis and recent soft U.S. economic data remain concerns for Mexico's economy\par \par \uc2 (Adds more comments and background in paragraphs 5-6 and 10-13.)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Erin McCarthy \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Tighter monetary policy in Mexico may be possible without raising interest rates, thanks to benign inflation, Bank of Mexico Deputy Gov. Manuel Sanchez said Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Since U.S. monetary policy is relatively accommodative, it makes Mexico's rate relatively more restrictive, which helps control inflation, Sanchez said in an interview with Dow Jones Newswires. "We don't have to move because inflation is coming down, and actually, now our monetary policy stance may be gradually looking a little tighter because inflation is coming down without moving anything."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Mexico left its overnight interest rate target unchanged at 4.5% last week. The bank has held the rate at that level for two years, and is widely expected to stay on hold until the second quarter of 2012.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This is primarily because inflationary pressures remain tame in Mexico, with consumer prices unchanged in June and annual inflation standing at 3.28%. Annual core inflation was at 3.18% last month.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 One thing that could put pressure on noncore inflation is gasoline subsidies, Sanchez said. The government sets the retail price of gasoline, and has been raising prices gradually, but not at the same rate that international prices have been rising. As a result, state oil monopoly \f28 \uc2 \f2 Petroleos Mexicanos\f28 \uc2 \f2  , which exports crude oil but imports gasoline, ends up subsidizing the fuel.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If the gap between domestic and international gasoline prices continues to widen, there is potential for sharper increases in Mexico, he said. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is monitoring that carefully so inflation expectations aren't "contaminated" and don't move away from the bank's inflation target, Sanchez said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  maintains an inflation target of 3%, plus or minus one percentage point.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Mexican peso's appreciation is also helping to keep inflation in check, Sanchez added. The peso has strengthened about 5% against the dollar since the beginning of 2011, according to CQG.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Though other regional central banks have taken steps to curb their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ' appreciation, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Mexico\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is known for a less \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventionist\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  buys up to $600 million a month via a system of put options that can be exercised when the peso is stronger than its 20-day average. Under the options, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  takes advantage of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strength to bolster foreign reserves.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sanchez said he doesn't see concern about the peso's strength, especially because Mexican exports continue to perform well, although the economy faces several external risks, particularly the euro zone's debt crisis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  continues to monitor European and foreign banks in Mexico closely, though "we are pretty much confident that their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are sound," he added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mexico has recently seen significant capital inflows, especially in its local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  government debt market, with foreign positioning in peso-denominated government debt at all-time highs. Sanchez expects capital inflows to continue, although an extreme external event could trigger a sudden reversal in flows.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Also, with Mexico's economy so closely linked to the U.S. "a weaker-than-expected U.S. economy is worrisome in the sense that it may induce a weaker-than-expected base of economic recovery in Mexico," Sanchez said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Erin McCarthy, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-2712; erin.mccarthy@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 --Anthony Harrup contributed to this article. [ 07-14-11 1331ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110714e77e0004m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
