\par\par \b \uc2 Mexico's Peso Ends Weaker At 11.6430 To The Dollar On Euro-Zone Worries\b0\par\par\uc2 By Amy Guthrie   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 398 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Mai 2011\par 22:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY (Dow Jones)--The Mexican peso weakened against the U.S. dollar Friday as euro-zone debt worries swayed investors away from high-yielding assets.\par \par \uc2 The peso was quoted closing at MXN11.6430 to the dollar in Mexico City versus MXN11.6250 at Thursday's close, according to Infosel.\par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks lost ground, while Mexico's local stock index eked out a 0.1% advance. The euro, which the peso frequently tracks, plunged against its major rivals Friday.\par \par \uc2 Given external dynamics such as E.U. periphery risk premiums and divergent monetary policies, analysts with BBVA said Friday that they expect the peso to trade within a wide range of MXN11.50-MXN11.80 in the near term.\par \par \uc2 They also believe that long peso positions have declined enough to make room for fresh bets on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "We believe USD/MXN now stands close to fair value and would expect it to stabilize over the medium and long-term so long as Banxico [\b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ] does not materially \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and foreign inflows materialize as we expect them to," the BBVA team said, adding that they expect the peso to finish the year at MXN11.90.\par \par \uc2 Foreign investment in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -denominated Mexican debt instruments remains near an all-time high in spite of recent risk aversion-inspired selling.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's macroeconomic panorama is also encouraging. Mexico's National Statistics Institute reported Thursday that the economy grew by 4.6% on the year in the first three months of 2011, marking its seventh consecutive quarter of expansion. The figure fell short of market expectations as the economy showed signs of slowing from last year's 5.4% expansion.\par \par \uc2 Even so, the Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  predicts that the economy will grow an enviable 4% to 5% this year, and private sector predictions remain high.\par \par \uc2 "We trust that economic activity will rebound in the second half of the year amid improved domestic demand, based on the positive trajectory of various consumer and investment factors," said Eduardo Gonzalez, analyst with Citibank's Mexican unit, Banamex.\par \par \uc2 Gonzalez sees Mexico's gross domestic product growing 4.8% this year.\par \par \uc2 By Amy Guthrie, Dow Jones Newswires; (5255) 5980-5177, amy.guthrie@dowjones.com [ 20-05-11 2035GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1027 1852   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110520e75k00154\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
