\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Brazil real shrugs off new tax; pesos mixed\b0\par\par\uc2 649 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 17:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Market shrugs off new Brazil tax on foreign borrowing\par \par \uc2 * Peru sol stable after worst slump in nearly two years\par \par \uc2 * Brazil real firms 0.48 pct, Mexico peso up 0.19 pct\par \par \uc2 (Recasts, adds comments)\par \par \uc2 By Michael O'Boyle\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, March 29 (Reuters) - Investors in Brazil tested the government's resolve to curb the appreciation of the real on Tuesday by driving the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher in the face of the latest measure meant to undermine its global appeal.\par \par \uc2 The real <BRBY> shrugged off the government's announcement of a new tax on foreign borrowing for loans of up to 360 days. [ID:nN29248075]\par \par \uc2 "The market was expecting something a bit more aggressive," said Diego Donadio, a strategist at \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  in Sao Paulo.\par \par \uc2 The real bid 0.48 percent stronger to 1.652 per dollar on the local spot market. That left it less than 0.6 percent from a 2-1/2 year high just past the 1.65 level that has been considered the finance ministry's unofficial limit.\par \par \uc2 Financial flows into Brazil have been tempted by the huge spread between interest rates in developed markets and Brazil's double-digit rates.\par \par \uc2 In October, Brazil tripled to 6 percent the so-called IOF tax it charges foreigners when they buy local bonds.\par \par \uc2 But analysts said the lack of a tax on locals was allowing domestic banks and companies to borrow abroad, invest in Brazil's high-yielding debt and pocket the difference between interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said market players would likely find ways around any new measures, short of a broad flat tax on all inflows.\par \par \uc2 "The impact of a strong measure would be severe and the government is not willing to take this risk," Donadio said. "The finance ministry could become more vocal, but it is hard to fight all this abundant liquidity from abroad," he added.\par \par \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  noted much of current and future inflows would likely be related to equity investments, which are only taxed at a rate of 2 percent.\par \par \uc2 RBS strategists said demand for upcoming Brazilian stock offerings could boost demand for the real, opening up the chance for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to trade in the 1.60 to 1.65 range.\par \par \uc2 The real has firmed more than 5 percent from a November low despite increased government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts.\par \par \uc2 STRONG SUPPORT\par \par \uc2 Mexico's peso <MXN=> firmed 0.19 percent to 11.9525 per dollar, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was tripped up at the key 11.95 per dollar level.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit its strongest in nearly 2-1/2 years this month on signs of recovery in the United States, which buys most of Mexico's exports.\par \par \uc2 Solid monthly U.S. payrolls data due this month could help push the peso toward new highs around 11.80 per dollar, traders said. The fact that \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not stepping up its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts could also help it draw in more flows, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 "The lack of direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in exchange policy ... and the fact that there are good economic data in Mexico, all this is providing strong support to the peso," said Rebeca Lizarraga, a strategist at Scotia Capital in Mexico City.\par \par \uc2 Chile's peso <CLP=CL> bid 0.33 percent weaker to 481.90 per dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been hurt by recent weakness in the price of copper, the country's main export.\par \par \uc2 Peru's sol stabilized after its biggest slump in nearly two years in the previous session after polls showed the left-wing nationalist presidential candidate leading the race. [ID:nN28200405] and [ID:nPOLLSPE]\par \par \uc2 The sol <PEN=PE> bid a slight 0.04 percent weaker to 2.814 per dollar to notch its weakest close since December.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Jean Luis Arce in Mexico City; Editing by Andrew Hay)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110401e73t001a9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
