\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Latam  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  firm, conviction seen weak\b0\par\par\uc2 633 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 19:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Mexico peso firms 0.59 pct, Chile peso up 0.33 pct\par \par \uc2 * Investors seem to rule out deepening EU debt crisis\par \par \uc2 * Brazil real up 0.18 pct as \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  eyed\par \par \uc2 By Michael O'Boyle\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 MEXICO\b0 \highlight\uc2  CITY, March 24 (Reuters) - Key Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  firmed on Thursday, supported by a weaker dollar and rising stock markets, but analysts noted a lack of conviction behind the gains amid a host of global risks.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's real hit a two-week high, while Mexico and Chile's pesos traded at their strongest in more than a week.\par \par \uc2 Demand for riskier assets like stocks and emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose despite worries about the ongoing violence in the oil-producing Middle East, fears that Portugal will need a bailout and concerns about Japan's nuclear crisis.\par \par \uc2 The cascade of the negative global events had slammed Latin American assets this month.\par \par \uc2 "The market took a breath over the last couple of weeks, the global backdrop deteriorated, but it hasn't changed significantly, so investors are willing to put some money to work," said David Beker, head Latin American\par \par \uc2 "But I wouldn't say these investors have a strong hand, people are willing to put on short-term positions, but not medium- or long-term positions," he added.\par \par \uc2 Japan's disasters could end up weighing down global growth, as could a sustained spike in oil prices or the expansion of Europe's debt crisis to a major economy like Spain.\par \par \uc2 Global investors have been taking advantage of recent declines in Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on global jitters to place fresh bets on the region's high-yielding debt.\par \par \uc2 Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  could see support from high prices for local commodities, like Chile's copper and Brazil's iron and soy, while expectations of further interest rate hikes will tempt global investors with higher yields.\par \par \uc2 "Latin America has high commodity prices and these guys are more willing to hike interest rates," said Win Thin, an emerging markets strategist at \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 Mexico and Colombia are oil exporters while Brazil is energy self-sufficient, so they may be able to better weather higher oil prices than Asia, where most countries import, Thin said.\par \par \uc2 While \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Brazil could limit gains, \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Chile may be poised to advance further.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's peso <MXN=><MEX01> firmed 0.59 percent to 11.9480, edging past the psychological 11.95 level, which could open the field for a run toward its nearly 2-1/2 year high hit earlier this month at 11.8585.\par \par \uc2 Local banks exercised $200 million in dollar put options on Thursday, exhausting the last of the $600 million a month sold by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Outstanding options can help contain the peso's gains, but with the monthly allotment used up, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will find less resistance to advance in the coming sessions.\par \par \uc2 Chile's peso <CLP=CL> bid 0.33 percent stronger to 479.70 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 Chile imports most of its crude, but strong copper prices can help offset the impact of higher oil costs, analysts said. Chile is the world's top copper producer.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's real <BRBY> firmed 0.18 percent to 1.656 per dollar, gaining ground for the fifth straight session and trading at its strongest since March 9.\par \par \uc2 The real is than 1 percent from a 2-1/2 year high hit earlier this month that stirred concerns of fresh moves by authorities to tame the real's strength, which is hurting local manufacturers.\par \par \uc2 Traders said the market was cautiously pushing the real stronger amid expectations that new measures could be announced.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Jose de Castro in Sao Paulo, Editing by Diane Craft)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110327e73o000kk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
