\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Mkt Debt,  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Strengthen As Risk Aversion Eases\b0\par\par\uc2 531 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 20:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -Moderation in risk aversion helps emerging market assets\par \par \uc2 -In Latin America, Brazilian real, debt gain strength\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Anjali Cordeiro \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Emerging-market debt strengthened and Latin American \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  gained ground as investors showed more willingness to take on risk as concerns about Japan's nuclear crisis abated temporarily.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The risk premium, or spread, on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 J.P. Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  Emerging Markets Bond Index Global was 7 basis points tighter, to 303 basis points over Treasurys. In price terms the index was up 0.08%. Latin American \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also climbed against the U.S. dollar, with \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  like the Mexican peso and the Brazilian real making gains.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Risk is back on today on less worry about Japan," said RBS strategist Flavia Cattan-Naslausky. Risky assets got beaten down early last week on worries about the potential for a global economic fallout from the earthquake in Japan.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Latin America, the Mexican peso gained, with the dollar recently down 0.69%, to MXN11.9853. Cattan-Naslausky said the peso tends to benefit when oil is rising, as the country is a net oil exporter. Also, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Mexico\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's policy of not \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  makes the peso an outperformer when risk assets do well.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Helping risk assets on Monday was a temporary easing of concerns about the situation in Japan. On Monday, the United Nations nuclear watchdog, the International Atomic Energy Agency, said that the situation at the Japanese earthquake- and tsunami-battered Fukushima Daiichi nuclear power plant is steadily improving, but risks to the downside still remain.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among emerging market assets, the Brazilian real also climbed, with the dollar down 0.38%, to BRL1.666. Brazil will cut an additional 500 million Brazilian reais ($300 million) in spending from its federal budget this year, the country's Planning Ministry said Monday. Brazil's credit was ten basis points tighter, to 175 basis points over Treasurys. In price term, Brazil's index was up 0.27% on the Embig.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chile's peso ended barely stronger versus the dollar Monday on improved investor sentiment, but pared most of its early gains. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ended at CLP480.50 to the dollar, versus Friday's close of CLP480.80, while trading in a range of CLP479.30 to CLP481.80.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some of the riskiest emerging market debt outperformed, with Argentina 12 basis points tighter, to 579 basis points over Treasurys. In price terms, its index was up 1.21%. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2  also revised its 2011 GDP growth forecast for Argentina to 6.8% from 5.6%, citing Friday's release of higher than expected real GDP figures for the fourth quarter of 2010. Venezuela was 22 basis points tighter to 1163 basis points over Treasurys. In price terms its index was up 1.01%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, China's yuan rose to its highest level against the U.S. dollar under the current system late Monday in Asia after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  set a record-low dollar/yuan central parity following its latest bank reserve hike.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Anjali Cordeiro; Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-2200; anjali.cordeiro@dowjones.com [ 21-03-11 1936GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110321e73l00169\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
