\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Bank Of  \highlight22\uc2 Mexico \highlight\uc2  To Still Avoid  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  -BBH\b0\par\par\uc2 1,301 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Februar 2011\par 16:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 10:51 (Dow Jones) \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely continue its non-\b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  approach to peso strength, \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2  says, adding that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation will help contain inflation pressures. After slipping last week on global market jitters over upheaval in Libya, the peso was recently quoted in Mexico City at MXN12.1005 vs US dollar, according to Infosel, compared with MXN12.1215 Friday. "The recent backup in USD/MXN to above 12.10 provides a good entry point for long peso positions, in our view," says BBH. Bank of Mexico seen holding rates at this week's monetary policy meeting. (anthony.harrup@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:41 (Dow Jones) Liberty Media reports 4Q subscriber gains at its premium cable movie network, Starz (LSTZA), that exceeded expectations. The performance provides ammunition for the company to use against critics who claim its streaming video deal with Netflix (NFLX) could lead pay-TV customers to drop their cable subscriptions for premium channels like Starz, HBO and Showtime. Starz's subscriber tally increased 800,000 during 4Q to 18.2M. The gain comes as HBO lost subscribers in 2010--a setback the company attributed to economic weakness and a lack of promotional efforts from satellite-TV providers. LSTZA up 4.1% to $71.67. (nat.worden@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:36 (Dow Jones) AES is seeing stronger-than-expected economic and power-demand growth in Latin America and Asia, which CEO Paul Hanrahan says is helping offset deteriorating conditions in North America. Weak natural gas prices and high coal costs continue to erode profitability in the region, which is eclipsed revenuewise by Latin America. Those woes have been impacting US power producers the past several years. AES up 1.7% at $12.47 even as 4Q EPS missed analysts' expectations. (naureen.malik@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:35 (Dow Jones) \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  upgrades the financial sector to what it calls a "small overweight," noting a recovery in returns on equity might finally be under way. "Despite all of the risks and the long-term effects of higher regulatory capital, we believe that the sector is approaching the point in the recovery process when return on equity can begin to climb back towards potential," firm says. \uc2 Barclays\uc2  thinks that in the not-so-distant future large banks might trade at 1.3 times to 1.5 times book value. "This won't necessarily happen in 2011," but outlook warrants an upgrade to marketweight plus from marketweight. (matthias.rieker@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:31 (Dow Jones) Sandler O'Neill cuts Jefferies (JEF) F1Q EPS estimate to 41c from 48c, reduces 2011 forecast to $1.60 from $1.65, as firm thinks JEF's likely to report lower-than-expected capital markets activity. While high-yield debt and equity trading volumes rebounded from December slowdown, firm says magnitude of improvement is "less than we had hoped for." Says investment-banking revenue should be resilient as equity underwriting will likely be up on strong volume, but fixed-income underwriting likely to decline, though by less than league tables would suggest. Advisory revenue likely down 5% sequentially, Sandler says, but helped by more attractive blend of reported deals. JEF down a fraction at $24.24. (brett.philbin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:30 (Dow Jones) Citi stock strategist Tobias Levkovich says not to head to the inflation bunker on a misremembering of the 1970s. Higher oil-and-gas prices don't necessarily result in inflation, though one X-factor is labor costs. During the '60s and '70s, nearly a third of the private sector was unionized, and cost-of-living wage adjustments that were built into contracts caused "a massive change in inflation expectations." Levkovich contends thinned-out unions are one reason the huge jump in crude prices from $9/barrel in 1998 to $147 in 2008 didn't bring an accompanying surge in CPI. (christopher.dieterich@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:24 (Dow Jones) Boston Federal Reserve President Eric Rosengren says it is assumed--perhaps incorrectly--that state and local finances won't be disruptive to the national economy. While most of the states having problems are wealthier ones and have the economic capacity to deal with their problems, the question is "will the politicians have the wherewithal to actually find a solution that won't be disruptive?" Rosengren also says in a speech, "While I expect these issues will be resolved without widespread or cascading problems, we should consider what scenarios could emerge if political impasses result in more disruptive outcomes." (bradley.davis@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:21 (Dow Jones) Amid being pressured by interest-rate expectations in Europe, the dollar could remain so as the week progresses with Bernanke addressing Capitol Hill starting Tuesday. Traders will be hanging on any dovish remarks, says Kathy Lien of GFT \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 , contending if the Fed chief suggests staying in an accomodative mindset "is even a small possibility," the dolalr "could head even lower." The euro was recently at $1.3824, up from 1.3754 late Friday, according to EBS via CQG. (andrewj.johnson@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:14 (Dow Jones) The turmoil in Libya could cause the country's oil-and-gas output to be shuttered for "months," says \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2 . It estimates up to 1.2M barrels of daily oil output has been lost following production halts by the country's largest oil producers. The investment bank says the disruption "could well be the 9th largest supply shock since 1950." Other OPEC countries, including Saudi Arabia and Kuwait, have signaled they will make up for any shortfall in crude supplies due to the revolt in Libya. April crude on Nymex trades down 75c at $97.13 while Brent falls 27c to $111.87. (dan.strumpf@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:08 (Dow Jones) Long-dated Treasurys are edging slightly lower as US stocks gain more ground. Pending home sales are basically in line with forecast, so that's not a mover for markets. The 10-year note is 2/32 lower to yield 3.424%. (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:06 (Dow Jones) The US economy is far from facing a stagflationary economic environment, but if inflation were to surge at a time of low growth, monetary policy wouldn't be easy or comfortable, says New York Fed President William Dudley. If price-pressure gains prove persistent "that would make the monetary policy more complicated because on one hand we'd be far away from our employment objective, but we'd have inflation above what we were seeking," he argues. But fortunately, "right now we are not in that circumstance," and "as long as we keep inflation expectations well anchored...I don't think we are going to have that problem." (michael.derby@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:04 (Dow Jones) Current softening in spot iron ore prices from recent record highs will be short-lived and won't hit Brazilian miner Vale's "very strong earnings momentum for 1H11," \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  says. \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  notes iron ore demand strength, from China and elsewhere, should push prices up again in 2Q as March-May typically the global steel industry's seasonal strong period, driven by construction. End to China's electricity rationing "adds further icing" to cake, CS says. \uc2 UBS\uc2  says VALE's iron ore pricing ahead of estimates as VALE sells ore together with freight package, boosting price gained per ton. In NY, VALE up a fraction at $34.33. (diana.kinch@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Equities Market Talk Editors, \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Paul Vigna, 212 416-2184,paul.vigna@dowjones.com \par Visit the Market Talk website at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.djnmarkettalk.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.djnmarkettalk.com}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 02-28-11 1051ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4035   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110228e72s000eq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
