\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: S&P/ASX 200 Likely Firmer; Focus On China PMI\b0\par\par\uc2 2,156 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Januar 2011\par 23:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 2256 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 is likely to trade firmer Tuesday after Monday's bullish reversal pattern was backed up by a 0.8% rise in the S&P 500, although that was boosted by strong results from \uc2 Exxon Mobil\uc2 . IG Markets tips 0.2% opening rise to 4764.0, with materials expected to recover, particularly if China's manufacturing PMI data beat expectations when released at 0100 GMT. However, S&P/ASX 200 will probably track the Shanghai Composite again--overly strong PMI data could rekindle tightening fears in China. RBA's policy decision at 0330 GMT will also be closely watched. Steady policy is unanimously expected and the RBA may endorse market views that rate hikes will be delayed until 2H. However, while S&P/ASX 200 may regain the support line from the ascending triangle pattern around 4760.0 today, leaving little resistance on the charts until the low 4800s, there are a couple of potential negatives to consider. A potential category 4 storm is bearing down on Queensland state and further flood damage is possible. Also, with Egyptian opposition groups planning a one-million person march against President Mubarak for Tuesday, the country's crisis could put further upward pressure on oil prices, which traded near multi-year highs last night. Index last 4753.9. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2256 GMT [Dow Jones] The Nikkei may trade in a 10,200-10,300 range Tuesday vs Monday's close of 10,237.92 (down 1.2%) with some buying back of exporter shares expected on the back of a firmer EUR/JPY (now Y112.38) and solid corporate earnings reports. "It may take time until we see a full-fledged rebound in shares," says Kazuhiro Takahashi, general manager at Daiwa Securities Capital Markets. He says investors are likely to take a wait-and-see stance amid continuing tensions in Egypt and as USD/JPY hovers at the lower Y82 level before a string of key U.S. economic data due later this week. Euro-linked shares such as \uc2 Sony\uc2  (6758.TO) may rebound following Monday's steep falls while oil-linked stocks may add to their Monday gains after crude prices settled at their highest level since October 2008. Nikkei 225 March futures ended Chicago trading at 10,250 vs the Osaka close at 10,230. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2255 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIA OUTLOOK: Regional stock markets are likely to be cheered by Wall Street's gains and better-than-expected U.S. economic data Monday; oil-related firms could benefit from the strength in crude prices, which were buoyed by the continued political turmoil in Egypt. Still, expect trade to thin out as many investors in Asia wind down activity ahead of the Lunar New Year holidays this week. Malaysian and Vietnamese markets are shut while Taiwan's stock and bond markets are closed. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the euro is likely to be supported by the improved risk appetite as well as growing speculation that the ECB may raise rates to curb inflation; the EUR/USD is at 1.3693 vs 1.3612 in late NY trade, the EUR/JPY is at 112.36 vs 112.35 and the USD/JPY is at 82.07 vs 82.04. On the data slate, Korea's CPI is at 2300 GMT, its manufacturing PMI is at 0000 GMT, Australian house price indexes and the NAB business survey are at 0030 GMT, China's CFLP manufacturing PMI is at 0100 GMT, Korea's trade data are at 0100 GMT, China's \uc2 HSBC\uc2  manufacturing PMI is at 0230 GMT, the RBA's cash rate decision is at 0330 GMT, Indonesia's CPI and trade balance are at 0400 GMT, India's manufacturing PMI is at 0500 GMT, Australia's commodity price index is at 0530 GMT, Thailand's CPI is at 0700 GMT, Hong Kong retail sales is at 0830 GMT and Singapore's PMI is at 1330 GMT. Later there's also the U.K.'s Nationwide housing review, the euro zone's manufacturing PMI, the BOE's lending to individuals, the U.K.'s CIPS manufacturing PMI, and the E.U.'s jobless rate. ECB head Trichet is due to speak. From the U.S. there's ICSC-\uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  chain store sales, the Johnson Redbook retail sales index, ISM manufacturing PMI, construction spending, global manufacturing PMI, the API oil report and the ABC News consumer comfort index. (colin.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2252 GMT [Dow Jones] New Zealand's 4Q labor cost index and quarterly employment survey show underlying wage pressures remain fairly modest and should present very little to concern the RBNZ, says ASB economist Jane Turner. "Labor demand indicators for the December quarter are very weak, in keeping with recent disappointing data." The labor cost index rose 0.6% on the quarter, matching market expectations. Turner adds labor demand indicators for 4Q were weak due to the patchy economic recovery. It "highlights the need for interest rates to remain low and stimulatory over much of 2011." She says the current weakness in activity was, however, expected to be temporary. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2225 GMT [Dow Jones] \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  volume in London and New York, the world's two biggest trading centers, surged to new records in October, marking another milestone in the global financial system's recovery. Although increasing cross-border transactions point to increasing \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  volumes over the long term, volatility over the past year has contributed to rising volumes in the short term, said Jeff Feig, managing director and global head of Group of 10 \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  and the chairman of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Committee sponsored by the New York Fed. The Fed's implementation of its $600 billion asset-purchase plan, along with the euro zone's long-smoldering sovereign debt crisis, has led to "significant volatility," Feig said in an interview with Dow Jones Newswires. If volatility falls, as Feig said it has so far in January, "I wouldn't be surprised to see volumes stay flat or even fall," in upcoming near-term surveys.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (robert.flint@dowjones.com) \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2225 GMT [Dow Jones] The AUD/USD is trading quietly ahead of the 0330 GMT RBA policy statement. Joe Capurso, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2 , says he expects a dovish statement from the RBA to accompany a decision that leaves interest rates on hold at 4.75%. Says the RBA must deal with the issue of the Queensland floods, which will slow the economy in the first half of 2011, leaving the market with the sense that rates won't rise any time soon. Expects the Australian dollar to fall by close to half a U.S. cent, giving back some of the gains it posted overnight on a recovery in risk appetite. AUD/USD latest 0.9973. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2215 GMT [Dow Jones] Despite increasing debate on whether \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Mexico\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or finance ministry will \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  remains a low short-term risk in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Mexico\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," writes \f28 \uc2 \f2 RBC Capital Markets\f28 \uc2 \f2 . The Treasury's economic policy director Miguel Messmacher "reinforced the long held non-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  view in the Treasury and Banxico, stating on Monday that the government does 'not see a problem' in the MXN's recent gains," it added. The Mexican peso is againing "flirting" with the MXN12.00 level, with USD/MXN at 12.1128, according to CQG. (erin.mccarthy@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in New York. 1-212-416-3100 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2210 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold is at $1,332.80/oz, up 50 cents early in Asia, after trading in a range of $1,323.40-$1,346.25. Gold futures on Comex in New York fell overnight as stronger U.S. economic data and easing concern over civil unrest in Egypt eroded the precious metal's safe-haven appeal. That said, gold is finding support from events in the Middle East which, for now, are offsetting liquidation from the exchange traded funds, according to Triland Metals. Liquidation from one ETF alone over the past week reached more than 47 metric tons, while the latest commitment of trade figures from Comex also indicate further liquidation mainly in gold and silver, Triland says. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2207 GMT [Dow Jones] New Zealand's economic recovery may have received a slight boost in the 4Q 2010 after ordinary-time salary and wage rates in the private sector rose 0.6% on quarter and were up 1.9% on year. However, the Quarterly Employment Survey, which was released with the LCI, showed 4Q filled jobs were up just 0.1% on quarter, although these were down 0.4% on year. Economists will be looking at the data closely for any clues the numbers might give on what to expect from Thursday's Household Labor Force Survey. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2205 GMT [Dow Jones] Mexico's peso recouped some of the previous session's losses, closing modestly stronger against the dollar as easing concern over unrest in Egypt allowed U.S. equities and other risk-sensitive assets to edge higher. USD/MXN closed in Mexico City at 12.1300 to the dollar, according to Infosel, compared with 12.1970 at Friday's close. The gains weren't quite enough to make up for the peso's steep slide Friday, when massive protests against Egyptian President Mubarak sparked a wave of risk aversion that sent USD/MXN soaring from 12.0260 at Thursday's close. "Today's relative calm has seen equity markets and [emerging-market] \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  recover some of last week's losses, but we acknowledge that event risk is skewed towards the downside until President Mubarak's intentions become known," said Win Thin, global head of emerging markets strategy at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Brown Brothers Harriman\f28 \uc2 \f2 , referring to the question of whether the Egyptian leader will resign.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (paul.kiernan@dowjones.com) \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 01-31-11 1756ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2204 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 offshore leads are bullish, with overnight SPI 200 futures up 0.3% at 4738.0 after better-than expected results from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Exxon Mobil\f28 \uc2 \f2  and stronger-than-expected U.S. economic data offset ongoing concern about political instability in Egypt. Emerging markets fell, as Egyptian opposition groups planned a million-person march against President Mubarak. U.S. December consumer spending rose 0.7% vs 0.5% expected and ISM's business index rose to 68.8 vs 64.5 expected. The DJIA rose 0.6% to 11891.93, S&P 500 rose 0.8% to 1286.12 and Nasdaq rose 0.5% to 2700.08. Intel fell 0.3% on a product recall, pushing \f28 \uc2 \f2 Advanced Micro Devices\f28 \uc2 \f2  up 4.5%. S&P 500 Financials rose 1.0%, while Materials rose 1.6%, with BHP Billiton (BHP.AU) ADRs at equivalent A$44.66 (based on AUD/USD at 0.9968) vs A$44.25 locally. S&P 500 Energy rose 2.6%, with Exxon up 2.1%. In commodity markets, Nymex crude oil rose US$2.85, or 3.2%, to US$89.34. Spot gold is at US$1,332.55, vs US$1,338.40 early Monday, while LME copper rose 2.3% to US$9.745 on sharply lower inventories. In \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, EUR/USD has risen to 1.3691 vs 1.3600 on higher-than-expected European inflation data. AUD/USD is at 0.9967 vs 0.9900. S&P/ASX 200 last 4753.9. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2150 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian milling wheat prices are likely to be well supported today after U.S. wheat futures climbed overnight as traders shrugged off concerns Egyptian protests would disrupt grain imports. Soft red winter wheat for March delivery on CBOT, the most-active contract, added 15 cents, or 1.8%, to settle at US$8.4075 a bushel, with prices recovering after falling 2.4% Friday. "Whenever a trader senses unrest in a market, they'll get out of positions. Then they'll start to come in when tensions ease, and that's what we're starting to see," says Brian Hoops, president of Midwest Market Solutions, a South Dakota-based commodities brokerage. AUD/USD at 0.9967 is near the upper end of a tidy rally from 0.9875 Monday morning and this strength could limit any gains for Australian wheat prices. On Monday, ASX milling wheat futures deliverable March in New South Wales rose A$3 to A$333/metric ton; ASX milling wheat futures deliverable March in Western Australia held unchanged at A$340. ray.brindal@dowjones.com) [ 01-31-11 1756ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110131e71v000dq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
