againing "flirting" with the MXN12.00 level, with USD/MXN at 12.1128, according to CQG. (erin.mccarthy@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in New York. 1-212-416-3100 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2210 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold is at $1,332.80/oz, up 50 cents early in Asia, after trading in a range of $1,323.40-$1,346.25. Gold futures on Comex in New York fell overnight as stronger U.S. economic data and easing concern over civil unrest in Egypt eroded the precious metal's safe-haven appeal. That said, gold is finding support from events in the Middle East which, for now, are offsetting liquidation from the exchange traded funds, according to Triland Metals. Liquidation from one ETF alone over the past week reached more than 47 metric tons, while the latest commitment of trade figures from Comex also indicate further liquidation mainly in gold and silver, Triland says. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2207 GMT [Dow Jones] New Zealand's economic recovery may have received a slight boost in the 4Q 2010 after ordinary-time salary and wage rates in the private sector rose 0.6% on quarter and were up 1.9% on year. However, the Quarterly Employment Survey, which was released with the LCI, showed 4Q filled jobs were up just 0.1% on quarter, although these were down 0.4% on year. Economists will be looking at the data closely for any clues the numbers might give on what to expect from Thursday's Household Labor Force Survey. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2205 GMT [Dow Jones] Mexico's peso recouped some of the previous session's losses, closing modestly stronger against the dollar as easing concern over unrest in Egypt allowed U.S. equities and other risk-sensitive assets to edge higher. USD/MXN closed in Mexico City at 12.1300 to the dollar, according to Infosel, compared with 12.1970 at Friday's close. The gains weren't quite enough to make up for the peso's steep slide Friday, when massive protests against Egyptian President Mubarak sparked a wave of risk aversion that sent USD/MXN soaring from 12.0260 at Thursday's close. "Today's relative calm has seen equity markets and [emerging-market] \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  recover some of last week's losses, but we acknowledge that event risk is skewed towards the downside until President Mubarak's intentions become known," said Win Thin, global head of emerging markets strategy at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Brown Brothers Harriman\f28 \uc2 \f2 , referring to the question of whether the Egyptian leader will resign.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (paul.kiernan@dowjones.com) \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2204 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 offshore leads are bullish, with overnight SPI 200 futures up 0.3% at 4738.0 after better-than expected results from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Exxon Mobil\f28 \uc2 \f2  and stronger-than-expected U.S. economic data offset ongoing concern about political instability in Egypt. Emerging markets fell, as Egyptian opposition groups planned a million-person march against President Mubarak. U.S. December consumer spending rose 0.7% vs 0.5% expected and ISM's business index rose to 68.8 vs 64.5 expected. The DJIA rose 0.6% to 11891.93, S&P 500 rose 0.8% to 1286.12 and Nasdaq rose 0.5% to 2700.08. Intel fell 0.3% on a product recall, pushing \f28 \uc2 \f2 Advanced Micro Devices\f28 \uc2 \f2  up 4.5%. S&P 500 Financials rose 1.0%, while Materials rose 1.6%, with BHP Billiton (BHP.AU) ADRs at equivalent A$44.66 (based on AUD/USD at 0.9968) vs A$44.25 locally. S&P 500 Energy rose 2.6%, with Exxon up 2.1%. In commodity markets, Nymex crude oil rose US$2.85, or 3.2%, to US$89.34. Spot gold is at US$1,332.55, vs US$1,338.40 early Monday, while LME copper rose 2.3% to US$9.745 on sharply lower inventories. In \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, EUR/USD has risen to 1.3691 vs 1.3600 on higher-than-expected European inflation data. AUD/USD is at 0.9967 vs 0.9900. S&P/ASX 200 last 4753.9. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2150 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian milling wheat prices are likely to be well supported today after U.S. wheat futures climbed overnight as traders shrugged off concerns Egyptian protests would disrupt grain imports. Soft red winter wheat for March delivery on CBOT, the most-active contract, added 15 cents, or 1.8%, to settle at US$8.4075 a bushel, with prices recovering after falling 2.4% Friday. "Whenever a trader senses unrest in a market, they'll get out of positions. Then they'll start to come in when tensions ease, and that's what we're starting to see," says Brian Hoops, president of Midwest Market Solutions, a South Dakota-based commodities brokerage. AUD/USD at 0.9967 is near the upper end of a tidy rally from 0.9875 Monday morning and this strength could limit any gains for Australian wheat prices. On Monday, ASX milling wheat futures deliverable March in New South Wales rose A$3 to A$333/metric ton; ASX milling wheat futures deliverable March in Western Australia held unchanged at A$340. ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 4:32 (Dow Jones) Fourth-quarter results will show renewed subscriber growth in the traditional pay-TV industry, according to Nomura Securities, which argues that declines of 2010 were driven mainly by the weak housing market and the after-effects of a government-mandated switch to a digital broadcast spectrum. Firm says Netflix's (NFLX) growth doesn't mean pay-TV subscriptions will shrink, noting that \f28 \uc2 \f2 Time Warner Cable\f28 \uc2 \f2 's (TWC) 4Q results showed the first increase in premium movie subs in almost three years. It says online distribution strategies in the TV industry are evolving to protect traditional channels, and rising programming costs for cable and satellite operators are likely to continue for years to come. (nat.worden@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Call us at (415) 439-6402 or email andrew.morse@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Visit the Market Talk website at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.djnmarkettalk.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.djnmarkettalk.com}}}\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2117 GMT [Dow Jones] Fed-funds futures stay close to Friday's level, pricing in about even odds for FOMC to begin tightening early next year. Reduced desire for safe havens, tied to Egypt's unrest and better-than-expected U.S. economimc data, failed to act as market moving catalysts. Feb 2012 fed-funds contract prices in 52% chance for FOMC to lift funds rate to 0.5% at its meeting in late Jan of next year. That's down slightly from 54% chance at Fri's settlement. Same contract priced in 64% chance on Thurs and 78% chance on Wed. (Howard.packowitz@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2110 GMT [Dow Jones] Eurodollar futures prices fell as strong economic data and calmer attitudes about Egypt's problem reduce the need for safety. Quarterly 2012 through 2014 contracts settled down 2 to 4.5 BP. Nearest 1-yr calendar spread a litttle wider as market sees short-term rates rising at a somewhat faster clip. Mar 2012 contract priced in a rate 52 BP higher than rate priced into Mar 2011 contract. Same spread at 50 BP on Fri. (Howard.packowitz@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2100 GMT [Dow Jones] Long-term Tsy futures prices settled down, pressured by reduced anxiety about Egypt unrest. Better-than-anticipated economic data and worries about inflation also weighed on contracts -- as shown by the hot prices paid index reading in this morning's ISM-Chicago business barometer. Mar 10-yr Tsy notes hit session low of 120-19+, near the 3.375% cash yield equivalent. Settled at 120-25+, down 11+/32. Mar classic Tsy bonds, covering 15 to 25-yr maturities, settled at 120-20, down 26/32. Mar ultra Tsy bonds, covering maturities 25 yrs and up, settled 29/32 lower at 123-05. (Howard.packowitz@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2054 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZX-50 is likely to open higher, following the U.S. market which is up 0.4%, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Forsyth Barr\f28 \uc2 \f2  investment advisor Suzanne Kinnaird. "However, there will be a lot of focus on the building numbers again today." Stats NZ said Monday residential building permits issued in December fell a seasonally adjusted 18.6% from November. This is likely to weigh further on Fletcher Building (FBU.NZ) and Cavalier (CAV.NZ). \f28 \uc2 \f2 Building Society Holdings Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (BSH.NZ), formed through the merger of Canterbury Building Society, Southern Cross Building Society and MARAC Finance, will begin trading Tuesday, but Kinnaird says the stock is initially likely to be relatively illiquid as shareholders get to grips with the new structure. The NZX-50 closed down 0.4% at 3338.74 Monday. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com) [ 01-31-11 1725ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110131e71v000dl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
